WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.648 --> 00:00:03.398
(dramatic music)

2
00:00:37.097 --> 00:00:40.100
We think about courage, it's like we all know what it is,

3
00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:45.100
we all admire it, we all know what it can do,

4
00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:47.290
and yet it's relatively rare.

5
00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:48.550
Like it's one of those weird things

6
00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:49.840
where it's like we're all in agreement

7
00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:51.100
that courage is important,

8
00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:53.480
and then we're all sort of looking around

9
00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:56.030
and being like, "Why aren't people more courageous?"

10
00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:58.999
We seem to ask ourselves that question less, right?

11
00:00:58.999 --> 00:00:59.832
You know what I mean?

12
00:00:59.832 --> 00:01:01.110
We all have strong opinions

13
00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:04.170
about the lack of courage of our elected officials

14
00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:06.870
or public figures or whatever,

15
00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:10.270
but we're very rarely holding ourselves

16
00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:13.908
to the standard that we're asking about.

17
00:01:13.908 --> 00:01:16.491
(upbeat music)

18
00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:34.393
Hey, everybody.

19
00:01:34.393 --> 00:01:37.020
Today we're gonna talk about virtue.

20
00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:38.330
More specifically,

21
00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:42.110
the virtue upon which all other virtues sit.

22
00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:44.500
And that is courage,

23
00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:48.610
the ability to rise above fear, to act in the face of fear.

24
00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:52.140
A skill I think is important in helping us navigate

25
00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:55.170
our increasingly fear-based world

26
00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:57.890
and a practice that is absolutely essential,

27
00:01:57.890 --> 00:02:01.650
crucial in the pursuit of living a meaningful life.

28
00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:05.010
My guest for this discourse is none other than Ryan Holiday,

29
00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:07.970
returning for his fourth appearance on the podcast.

30
00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:10.580
Ryan is best known for pioneering Stoicism

31
00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:14.130
to mainstream adoption and is considered

32
00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:18.260
one of the world's bestselling living philosophers.

33
00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:20.140
His expertize is coveted

34
00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:22.640
by some of the world's most successful people.

35
00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:26.427
And his books, which include "The Obstacle Is the Way,"

36
00:02:26.427 --> 00:02:28.017
"Ego Is the Enemy,"

37
00:02:28.017 --> 00:02:29.017
"The Daily Stoic,"

38
00:02:29.017 --> 00:02:30.710
"Stillness is the Key,"

39
00:02:30.710 --> 00:02:32.040
and his latest offering

40
00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:34.067
and the focus of today's conversation,

41
00:02:34.067 --> 00:02:37.420
"Courage Is Calling" right here

42
00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:41.880
have sold altogether over four million copies.

43
00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:43.960
I always relish my conversations with Ryan.

44
00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:48.230
He is a compelling thinker about things that matter.

45
00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:51.240
This one is chockablock with practical wisdom

46
00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:55.080
and essentially is about what we can learn from philosophy

47
00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:57.750 line:15%
and history with respect to virtue

48
00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:01.060 line:15%
to live better, more meaningful lives.

49
00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:02.960 line:15%
So, let's do it.

50
00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:04.993 line:15%
This is me and Ryan Holiday.

51
00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:06.893
Right on.

52
00:03:06.893 --> 00:03:07.860
Ryan Holiday in the house.

53
00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:08.693
Good to see you, man.

54
00:03:08.693 --> 00:03:10.390 line:15%
Yeah, it's been two years.

55
00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:11.223 line:15%
Has it?

56
00:03:11.223 --> 00:03:12.213 line:15%
It's been two years.

57
00:03:12.213 --> 00:03:15.300 line:15%
It's a weird thing where time seems to move really slow

58
00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:17.150 line:15%
and really fast simultaneously.

59
00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:20.890
So, I can't remember things that happened yesterday

60
00:03:20.890 --> 00:03:23.620
and things that happened two years ago feel like yesterday.

61
00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:26.700
Yeah, there's a James Salter novel in the title,

62
00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:27.720
it's like his memoir.

63
00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:28.700
I guess, is a memoir.

64
00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:30.900
But the title is "Burning the Days."

65
00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:32.980
And that's like a phrase I've thought a lot about

66
00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:34.630
during the pandemic, that it just feels like

67
00:03:34.630 --> 00:03:38.880
they're all just like burning together kind of.

68
00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:40.900
Like it's like it could be last week

69
00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:42.460
or it could have been two years ago,

70
00:03:42.460 --> 00:03:46.860
and it all feels roughly the same distance from each other.

71
00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:50.500
I feel like this deep sense of irony,

72
00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:52.010
in that the last time that we spoke,

73
00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:54.830
it was about your book, "Stillness Is the Key."

74
00:03:54.830 --> 00:03:56.470
And in the wake of that conversation,

75
00:03:56.470 --> 00:03:58.470
we were met with the pandemic.

76
00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:02.880
And that compelled us into this forced repose,

77
00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:05.930
where we had the opportunity to engage with stillness,

78
00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:08.170
to really reckon with what's most important,

79
00:04:08.170 --> 00:04:12.409
to discard that which no longer serves us and all of that.

80
00:04:12.409 --> 00:04:15.470
And you would imagine with that,

81
00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:19.130
that we would emerge better human beings.

82
00:04:19.130 --> 00:04:23.490
And yet I feel like we're more divided, more separated,

83
00:04:23.490 --> 00:04:24.770
more acrimonious.

84
00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:28.510
I feel like we're teetering on kind of

85
00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:30.430
like social collapse at the moment

86
00:04:30.430 --> 00:04:34.490
as opposed to what we could have kind of learned

87
00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:35.930
throughout this period.

88
00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:37.800
Well, people are often very flippant

89
00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:39.630
about like meditation, for instance.

90
00:04:39.630 --> 00:04:41.470
Like everyone should meditate.

91
00:04:41.470 --> 00:04:44.810
And you realize like that spending time alone

92
00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:45.900
with your thoughts,

93
00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:48.610
may be like the absolute worst prescription

94
00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:51.540
for a certain type or a group of people.

95
00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:56.540
And so, while some of us used the last 18 months

96
00:04:56.690 --> 00:04:58.080
to reflect and grow

97
00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:01.500
and like reevaluate lives and priorities,

98
00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:04.720
you can tell that a lot of people came,

99
00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:06.060
I sort of liken it to it's like

100
00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:11.060
you catch a flash of truth or yourself inside,

101
00:05:14.170 --> 00:05:16.540
and you can look at it and face it,

102
00:05:16.540 --> 00:05:20.060
or you can turn away and run the opposite direction.

103
00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:23.340
And I think some people,

104
00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:24.880
as the world slowed down

105
00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:28.723
and they were forced to look at like everything,

106
00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:32.370
decided, "I don't wanna look at that,

107
00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:35.070
because if I look at it, then I have to make changes."

108
00:05:36.140 --> 00:05:38.720
And so, maybe that's why they went down this rabbit hole

109
00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:41.270
or they got consumed with this.

110
00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:44.220
I also think it's why a lot of people moved

111
00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:46.750
or quit their jobs or got divorced.

112
00:05:46.750 --> 00:05:51.750
It's just like anything to not have to sit quietly

113
00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:52.840
with your own thoughts.

114
00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:54.300
Which is terrifying.

115
00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:55.437
Yeah.

116
00:05:55.437 --> 00:05:56.270
I mean, there's that Blaise Pascal quote.

117
00:05:56.270 --> 00:05:59.730
He says, like to sit quietly in a room alone

118
00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:00.949
is like the hardest thing

119
00:06:00.949 --> 00:06:02.048
that a human being can possibly do.

120
00:06:02.048 --> 00:06:03.070
Right, or is it he said something

121
00:06:03.070 --> 00:06:04.883
like maybe it's the same quote,

122
00:06:06.290 --> 00:06:11.290
that the summation of suffering

123
00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:14.240
can be drilled down to man's inability

124
00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:15.607
to sit alone with himself.

125
00:06:15.607 --> 00:06:18.327
Yeah, I think that is the quote.

126
00:06:18.327 --> 00:06:20.700
It's like instead of...

127
00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:22.267
Like doing nothing is actually like the hardest thing

128
00:06:22.267 --> 00:06:23.840
in the world.

129
00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:28.170
And so, people found,

130
00:06:28.170 --> 00:06:29.540
I mean, some of it is positive,

131
00:06:29.540 --> 00:06:32.090
they learned how to bake bread, but also-

132
00:06:32.090 --> 00:06:34.030
Yeah, but that had its own shelf life.

133
00:06:34.030 --> 00:06:35.920
We're quickly tired of that.

134
00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:40.200
I think you also have to layer on top of that,

135
00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:43.380
a very real fear of getting sick

136
00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:46.240
and then all of the stressors and anxiety

137
00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:48.860
that gets packed into the uncertainty of the moment,

138
00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:51.520
which, of course, without tools

139
00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:55.470
is going to manifest in bad behavior all across the board.

140
00:06:55.470 --> 00:07:00.150
Well, I think at the root of all unstillness

141
00:07:00.150 --> 00:07:03.610
is that sort of vague floating fear of death.

142
00:07:03.610 --> 00:07:05.410
Like what does it all mean?

143
00:07:05.410 --> 00:07:07.480
How long do I have?

144
00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:09.640
Where do I go after I die?

145
00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:13.310
And so, there's nothing quite like a deadly virus

146
00:07:13.310 --> 00:07:17.880
floating through the air, randomly picking off people,

147
00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:20.720
people that you know, people that you've heard of,

148
00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:23.520
in enormous statistical numbers

149
00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:25.960
that are posted online every day

150
00:07:25.960 --> 00:07:30.960
to sort of stir up all of those feelings of restlessness

151
00:07:31.690 --> 00:07:33.150
and fear and anxiety.

152
00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:38.150
Yeah, and of course, fear expressed is anger, resentment,

153
00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:42.010
all the kind of discord that we're seeing

154
00:07:42.010 --> 00:07:46.320
being sown across every kind of sector of humanity.

155
00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:48.750
Well, yeah, it takes an immense amount of self-awareness

156
00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:52.000
to go like, "I'm feeling discomfort because of X,"

157
00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:55.970
or, "I'm feeling anxious because of X."

158
00:07:55.970 --> 00:07:57.030
I think that was something for me

159
00:07:57.030 --> 00:07:58.080
that I found in the pandemic,

160
00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:01.230
where suddenly, I wasn't doing anything.

161
00:08:01.230 --> 00:08:03.860
So, I wasn't having to get to this plane.

162
00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:05.810
I wasn't stuck in traffic here.

163
00:08:05.810 --> 00:08:08.450
I wasn't having to prepare for this or that.

164
00:08:08.450 --> 00:08:11.930
And so, you'd think that my anxiety would go way down.

165
00:08:11.930 --> 00:08:14.470
That suddenly you'd have a lot less to worry about.

166
00:08:14.470 --> 00:08:17.440
And then actually, that's not true.

167
00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:20.240
And then you realize, oh, the anxiety has nothing to do

168
00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:21.280
with any of the things.

169
00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:23.270
It's actually, Marcus Aurelius talks about this

170
00:08:23.270 --> 00:08:24.170
in meditations.

171
00:08:24.170 --> 00:08:26.630
He says, like, "Oh, the anxiety,"

172
00:08:26.630 --> 00:08:28.400
he says, "I escaped anxiety."

173
00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:31.050
And then he goes, "No, actually, I discarded it."

174
00:08:31.050 --> 00:08:32.990
And he writes this during a plague, more or less.

175
00:08:32.990 --> 00:08:37.610
But he goes, I discarded it because it was within me.

176
00:08:37.610 --> 00:08:39.407
And then so that was a breakthrough I sort of had.

177
00:08:39.407 --> 00:08:43.170
It was like, oh, I thought I was stressed and anxious

178
00:08:43.170 --> 00:08:47.640
and worried because of all of these very reasonable things

179
00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:52.140
that cause those things in your life, work, family, stuff.

180
00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:55.260
And then when all that gets paired down,

181
00:08:55.260 --> 00:08:58.120
you realize it's like, "Oh, no, it was me."

182
00:08:58.120 --> 00:08:59.200
I'm the common variable.

183
00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:00.960
Yeah, the anxiety is free-floating, right?

184
00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:04.130
And when you occupy yourself through travel and work

185
00:09:04.130 --> 00:09:05.920
and all the things that we do,

186
00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:09.520
those are ultimately distractions from the anxiety

187
00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:13.060
and not necessarily the thing that's provoking the anxiety.

188
00:09:13.060 --> 00:09:16.160
The anxiety exists independent of that.

189
00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:19.700
And left to our own devices and compelled to sit still,

190
00:09:19.700 --> 00:09:23.390
reckoning with that becomes a challenge, right?

191
00:09:23.390 --> 00:09:24.490
Yeah. Yeah.

192
00:09:24.490 --> 00:09:28.140
And when suddenly you can't express yourself

193
00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:31.657
through accomplishments or dizziness or activity-

194
00:09:31.657 --> 00:09:34.330
And your identity being wrapped up in that,

195
00:09:34.330 --> 00:09:35.560
I plead guilty to that.

196
00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:36.393
Of course.

197
00:09:36.393 --> 00:09:38.790
No, again, to have to slow down,

198
00:09:38.790 --> 00:09:40.490
I think for me, it was like to have to slow down

199
00:09:40.490 --> 00:09:42.763
and just do the work.

200
00:09:44.130 --> 00:09:47.430
Like just do like it's like, oh, I am...

201
00:09:47.430 --> 00:09:50.000
Weirdly, the good part about the pandemic

202
00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:52.330
was I was much closer to what I should be doing

203
00:09:52.330 --> 00:09:53.163
day in and day out,

204
00:09:53.163 --> 00:09:55.960
which is like wake up, spend time with my family,

205
00:09:55.960 --> 00:09:58.250
take care of my health, and write.

206
00:09:58.250 --> 00:09:59.430
Write books.

207
00:09:59.430 --> 00:10:00.263
Yeah.

208
00:10:00.263 --> 00:10:02.080
Not almost all the other stuff was impossible

209
00:10:02.080 --> 00:10:04.770
or if not illegal for short periods of time, right?

210
00:10:04.770 --> 00:10:07.520
And it's like, okay, so I can do that.

211
00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:09.790
And it's funny, though,

212
00:10:09.790 --> 00:10:12.570
because now, it's like I want things to go back to normal,

213
00:10:12.570 --> 00:10:14.864
that's what we say.

214
00:10:14.864 --> 00:10:17.460
And like normal is what caused this.

215
00:10:17.460 --> 00:10:18.700
Yeah.

216
00:10:18.700 --> 00:10:21.860
But now I think we're all understanding

217
00:10:21.860 --> 00:10:26.860
that we're never gonna return back to that idea of normal.

218
00:10:27.690 --> 00:10:30.120
We're gonna have to frame a new normal.

219
00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:32.400
And what we're contending with

220
00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:35.260
is something that is probably going to persist

221
00:10:35.260 --> 00:10:38.600
and be some component of our life or lifestyle

222
00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:40.770
for who knows how long.

223
00:10:40.770 --> 00:10:41.820
Yeah.

224
00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:46.570
And I mean, I think the thing that people struggle with

225
00:10:46.570 --> 00:10:48.252
the most when it comes to Stoicism,

226
00:10:48.252 --> 00:10:52.910
it's often associated with like sort of resignation.

227
00:10:52.910 --> 00:10:55.230
Like the Stoics use the word ascent.

228
00:10:55.230 --> 00:10:58.160
Not like ascend up a mountain, but A-S-S-E-N-T.

229
00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:00.970
Like I ascent to this.

230
00:11:00.970 --> 00:11:02.830
Which I actually think is a really important

231
00:11:02.830 --> 00:11:04.723
and poorly understood concept.

232
00:11:06.040 --> 00:11:07.410
Surely in the ancient world,

233
00:11:07.410 --> 00:11:09.023
we had a lot less agency over things.

234
00:11:09.023 --> 00:11:10.820
Like we were born in a certain class,

235
00:11:10.820 --> 00:11:13.090
you lived in a certain place.

236
00:11:13.090 --> 00:11:17.840
Your life expectancy was much lower tragedy, tyranny,

237
00:11:17.840 --> 00:11:18.980
all of these things,

238
00:11:18.980 --> 00:11:22.080
these larger forces than you exerted a lot more influence

239
00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:23.720
over your life.

240
00:11:23.720 --> 00:11:27.130
So, as we've rightfully broken out of that,

241
00:11:27.130 --> 00:11:28.210
you can get to a place,

242
00:11:28.210 --> 00:11:30.360
and I know you know this from recovery work,

243
00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:33.370
which is like you start to think like not only

244
00:11:33.370 --> 00:11:36.347
is there no higher power, you are the higher power.

245
00:11:36.347 --> 00:11:37.540
Like you're in control.

246
00:11:37.540 --> 00:11:41.457
And so, you get you have trouble with the idea of ascent.

247
00:11:41.457 --> 00:11:43.723
And I think even as we were looking at this with COVID,

248
00:11:43.723 --> 00:11:46.630
there was this brief moment not that long ago,

249
00:11:46.630 --> 00:11:50.040
where it was like, we have beaten this, it is going away.

250
00:11:50.040 --> 00:11:52.620
And now we're reckoning with the idea of like,

251
00:11:52.620 --> 00:11:53.900
no, it's endemic.

252
00:11:53.900 --> 00:11:55.520
It's here forever.

253
00:11:55.520 --> 00:11:57.080
And I've been thinking about that.

254
00:11:57.080 --> 00:12:01.650
It's just like, first off, the unpleasantness of it.

255
00:12:01.650 --> 00:12:03.620
Second, the unfairness of it.

256
00:12:03.620 --> 00:12:04.650
It's not my fault.

257
00:12:04.650 --> 00:12:06.660
I feel like I made all the right choices.

258
00:12:06.660 --> 00:12:08.150
I did what I was supposed to do.

259
00:12:08.150 --> 00:12:11.207
And yet here we are, that idea of just like,

260
00:12:11.207 --> 00:12:13.803
and so it goes like this is it.

261
00:12:14.680 --> 00:12:16.170
You just have to assent to it.

262
00:12:16.170 --> 00:12:19.180
Yeah, it's a graduate course.

263
00:12:19.180 --> 00:12:22.810
In what in recovery parlance is called surrender, right?

264
00:12:22.810 --> 00:12:27.810
Which on the subject of ascent being misunderstood,

265
00:12:28.210 --> 00:12:31.560
the idea of surrender being likened to giving up,

266
00:12:31.560 --> 00:12:32.740
which is not the case.

267
00:12:32.740 --> 00:12:34.740
It's really just an honest reckoning

268
00:12:34.740 --> 00:12:37.061
with what you have control over and what you don't have.

269
00:12:37.061 --> 00:12:38.900
These are the facts on the ground.

270
00:12:38.900 --> 00:12:42.430
Yeah, the idea that when you look at it objectively

271
00:12:42.430 --> 00:12:43.690
and truthfully,

272
00:12:43.690 --> 00:12:46.420
there is almost nothing that you can control.

273
00:12:46.420 --> 00:12:49.220
All you can control is how you comport yourself,

274
00:12:49.220 --> 00:12:51.570
how you respond to the world around you,

275
00:12:51.570 --> 00:12:53.810
how you react to the environment,

276
00:12:53.810 --> 00:12:55.080
the thoughts that you entertain,

277
00:12:55.080 --> 00:12:57.690
the people that you choose to surround yourself with,

278
00:12:57.690 --> 00:13:01.870
and everything else elude your ability to manage.

279
00:13:01.870 --> 00:13:04.680
And I think really embracing that

280
00:13:04.680 --> 00:13:07.000
creates a certain kind of freedom

281
00:13:07.000 --> 00:13:09.270
that makes you stronger and more capable.

282
00:13:09.270 --> 00:13:13.560
And that idea, which was very difficult for me to understand

283
00:13:13.560 --> 00:13:16.810
and learn as somebody who was relying on self-will

284
00:13:16.810 --> 00:13:18.610
for everything throughout my whole life,

285
00:13:18.610 --> 00:13:22.433
and in addition, they call it self-will runs riot,

286
00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:25.280
it took me a long time

287
00:13:25.280 --> 00:13:29.720
to really not only intellectualize what that meant

288
00:13:29.720 --> 00:13:31.040
and then to start living it,

289
00:13:31.040 --> 00:13:33.910
but when you're able to do that,

290
00:13:33.910 --> 00:13:36.490
you become so much more competent

291
00:13:36.490 --> 00:13:39.210
in every facet of your life.

292
00:13:39.210 --> 00:13:43.150
And it segues completely with the idea of Stoicism.

293
00:13:43.150 --> 00:13:46.110
Yeah, well, it's also just a resource allocation issue.

294
00:13:46.110 --> 00:13:47.895
So, like if I wake up tomorrow

295
00:13:47.895 --> 00:13:51.290
and I'm like, "It's unfair that it's this way.

296
00:13:51.290 --> 00:13:53.170
It sucks that it's this way.

297
00:13:53.170 --> 00:13:55.930
I wish that it was this other way."

298
00:13:55.930 --> 00:13:57.758
Or it should be a certain way.

299
00:13:57.758 --> 00:14:02.033
Yes, all of that is just not being directed at.

300
00:14:03.490 --> 00:14:05.190
I actually gave a virtual talk this morning.

301
00:14:05.190 --> 00:14:06.430
I was sort of stumbling over,

302
00:14:06.430 --> 00:14:08.547
and I accidentally I was like, so.

303
00:14:08.547 --> 00:14:10.620
And then I was like, so what?

304
00:14:10.620 --> 00:14:13.604
And I was like, so what are you going to do about it?

305
00:14:13.604 --> 00:14:15.305
Like it's just that's sort of how a stoic

306
00:14:15.305 --> 00:14:16.330
would think about it.

307
00:14:16.330 --> 00:14:18.810
It's like you can list this long list of problems

308
00:14:18.810 --> 00:14:20.690
and then like so what?

309
00:14:20.690 --> 00:14:23.260
And then so what?

310
00:14:23.260 --> 00:14:25.152
And then so what are you gonna do about it?

311
00:14:25.152 --> 00:14:30.152
That's where this idea of ascent intersects with will,

312
00:14:30.760 --> 00:14:34.120
because it's not like you want no will, no self-will.

313
00:14:34.120 --> 00:14:35.190
You have to have some of it.

314
00:14:35.190 --> 00:14:36.530
You wouldn't be where you were

315
00:14:36.530 --> 00:14:41.530
if you weren't able to make the most of the things

316
00:14:42.450 --> 00:14:43.973
you do have control over,

317
00:14:43.973 --> 00:14:46.721
but that the sort of intersection between what's up to you

318
00:14:46.721 --> 00:14:48.717
and what's not up to you,

319
00:14:48.717 --> 00:14:50.680
and then what you do with what's up to you,

320
00:14:50.680 --> 00:14:52.140
that's the whole thing.

321
00:14:52.140 --> 00:14:52.973
Right.

322
00:14:52.973 --> 00:14:54.440
Well, one of the things that you've done

323
00:14:54.440 --> 00:14:56.540
over the course of the past year

324
00:14:56.540 --> 00:15:00.090
that I'm really interested in hearing from you on

325
00:15:00.090 --> 00:15:04.244
is this bold pivot, which I think you could characterize

326
00:15:04.244 --> 00:15:07.880
as sort of a courageous step.

327
00:15:07.880 --> 00:15:09.403
We're gonna get into courage in a little bit.

328
00:15:09.403 --> 00:15:10.236
Okay.

329
00:15:10.236 --> 00:15:14.280
Is opening up this bookstore in a small town

330
00:15:14.280 --> 00:15:15.670
in the middle of a pandemic.

331
00:15:15.670 --> 00:15:17.050
I mean, we did our version of that

332
00:15:17.050 --> 00:15:18.480
by moving into this studio.

333
00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:21.060
Like when everyone is zigging, you zag,

334
00:15:21.060 --> 00:15:26.060
you try to find the opportunity in the setback, so to speak.

335
00:15:26.210 --> 00:15:31.140
And you've told the story of the bookstore on YouTube,

336
00:15:31.140 --> 00:15:33.180
which I've really enjoyed following.

337
00:15:33.180 --> 00:15:34.930
But tell me a little bit about the thinking

338
00:15:34.930 --> 00:15:38.180
behind why you decided to do that and how that's been.

339
00:15:38.180 --> 00:15:40.200
Well, so actually, when I saw you last,

340
00:15:40.200 --> 00:15:42.860
this is when I was thinking about doing it.

341
00:15:42.860 --> 00:15:45.090
So, when I was on my book tour for "Stillness,"

342
00:15:45.090 --> 00:15:46.660
one of the things I was...

343
00:15:46.660 --> 00:15:49.570
I'd seen the space that I was interested in.

344
00:15:49.570 --> 00:15:50.403
And then I was like,

345
00:15:50.403 --> 00:15:52.070
"Well, I'm gonna go around all these bookstores,

346
00:15:52.070 --> 00:15:54.200
like I'll do some research."

347
00:15:54.200 --> 00:15:56.610
And my wife and I had been thinking

348
00:15:56.610 --> 00:16:00.070
about sort of setting up shop like literally

349
00:16:00.070 --> 00:16:02.590
in the sense that either I worked out of my house

350
00:16:02.590 --> 00:16:04.670
or at an office in Austin.

351
00:16:04.670 --> 00:16:07.190
We live a little bit outside Austin.

352
00:16:07.190 --> 00:16:10.020
And we needed something sort of more central.

353
00:16:10.020 --> 00:16:11.410
But then also that was like a hub

354
00:16:11.410 --> 00:16:13.300
for all the stuff that I do.

355
00:16:13.300 --> 00:16:18.300
And we sort of made this crazy leap in

356
00:16:18.360 --> 00:16:23.010
I guess we closed on it, like in December of 2019,

357
00:16:23.010 --> 00:16:24.820
and then started getting serious about it

358
00:16:24.820 --> 00:16:28.450
in January and February of 2020.

359
00:16:28.450 --> 00:16:31.553
So, like the absolute worst timing you could imagine.

360
00:16:32.480 --> 00:16:34.440
I think we hired the first person for the bookstore,

361
00:16:34.440 --> 00:16:38.080
like two weeks before like everything shut down

362
00:16:38.080 --> 00:16:39.193
for the pandemic.

363
00:16:40.240 --> 00:16:42.740
But it sort of goes to also the idea of ascent, right?

364
00:16:42.740 --> 00:16:44.320
You chose to do something,

365
00:16:44.320 --> 00:16:46.990
you wrote the check or you sign the contract

366
00:16:46.990 --> 00:16:48.027
and then life's like,

367
00:16:48.027 --> 00:16:50.710
"Oh, you thought it was gonna be this hard?

368
00:16:50.710 --> 00:16:52.900
Well, now it's 20 times harder.

369
00:16:52.900 --> 00:16:54.908
What are you gonna do?"

370
00:16:54.908 --> 00:16:56.150
Like you started the race

371
00:16:56.150 --> 00:16:58.180
and then you lose one of your shoes or something

372
00:16:58.180 --> 00:17:00.440
and you have to decide like are you gonna quit,

373
00:17:00.440 --> 00:17:03.427
or you're gonna be like, "I'm just doing it this way now."

374
00:17:04.860 --> 00:17:06.849
But I think for me, the idea of a bookstore

375
00:17:06.849 --> 00:17:11.200
is like I love books and I think they're important.

376
00:17:11.200 --> 00:17:13.023
I love physical books, most of all.

377
00:17:16.420 --> 00:17:19.930
How could I with the success that I've had,

378
00:17:19.930 --> 00:17:23.080
make like sort of a positive contribution

379
00:17:23.080 --> 00:17:28.080
to a place that I both love and I'm a resident of?

380
00:17:28.140 --> 00:17:31.650
So, we opened this tiny bookstore in the main street,

381
00:17:31.650 --> 00:17:34.400
about 30 minutes from Austin

382
00:17:34.400 --> 00:17:37.343
in the building that's been there since the 1880s.

383
00:17:37.343 --> 00:17:39.200
Yes, like this historic main street.

384
00:17:39.200 --> 00:17:41.197
Yeah, it's basically like Mayberry.

385
00:17:41.197 --> 00:17:43.593
Like it's this tiny little town.

386
00:17:45.010 --> 00:17:47.550
My son, when we were going to school,

387
00:17:47.550 --> 00:17:49.772
was like right up the street.

388
00:17:49.772 --> 00:17:54.123
It's just this old school sort of Americana thing.

389
00:17:56.050 --> 00:17:59.963
And I think I do hope there is something.

390
00:17:59.963 --> 00:18:02.220
Like I am seeing it more commonly,

391
00:18:02.220 --> 00:18:04.580
where like people whose lives,

392
00:18:04.580 --> 00:18:07.940
like yours and mine that's like so internet-focused,

393
00:18:07.940 --> 00:18:09.894
so like scale-focused.

394
00:18:09.894 --> 00:18:12.327
Like you do a podcast, you reach millions of people.

395
00:18:12.327 --> 00:18:14.730
You do a YouTube video and it reaches millions of people.

396
00:18:14.730 --> 00:18:18.655
Your books, even most of the sales are digital, right?

397
00:18:18.655 --> 00:18:19.488
Yeah.

398
00:18:19.488 --> 00:18:20.321
And they're all over the world

399
00:18:20.321 --> 00:18:22.200
and you don't interact or see any of that.

400
00:18:22.200 --> 00:18:23.750
And I think part of it was just like,

401
00:18:23.750 --> 00:18:26.570
what if we did something like real?

402
00:18:26.570 --> 00:18:28.830
It could be at a much smaller scale,

403
00:18:28.830 --> 00:18:30.720
but it was real and physical

404
00:18:30.720 --> 00:18:33.540
and it actually like involved people.

405
00:18:33.540 --> 00:18:35.407
And then, of course, a pandemic comes around and says,

406
00:18:35.407 --> 00:18:38.590
"Actually, no, you can't have any people."

407
00:18:38.590 --> 00:18:40.600
So, that's been sort of a struggle and adjustment,

408
00:18:40.600 --> 00:18:42.370
but it's been cool.

409
00:18:42.370 --> 00:18:43.203
Well, the thing about it

410
00:18:43.203 --> 00:18:46.790
is that it can be this HQ for all this stuff that you do,

411
00:18:46.790 --> 00:18:47.623
as you mentioned.

412
00:18:47.623 --> 00:18:49.230
So, you need a place to work, to write.

413
00:18:49.230 --> 00:18:50.750
So, you have that there.

414
00:18:50.750 --> 00:18:52.120
You're now doing a podcast,

415
00:18:52.120 --> 00:18:55.180
which I wanna talk to you about that a little bit.

416
00:18:55.180 --> 00:18:57.240
You can do your podcasts there

417
00:18:57.240 --> 00:18:58.620
and you have this retail store.

418
00:18:58.620 --> 00:19:00.678
I think you didn't use sublease like the other half of it

419
00:19:00.678 --> 00:19:02.797
so that you could like kind of alleviate

420
00:19:02.797 --> 00:19:04.934
the overhead cost of the whole thing.

421
00:19:04.934 --> 00:19:06.310
Well, the other half was gonna be like events.

422
00:19:06.310 --> 00:19:08.166
That's what I was gonna use before.

423
00:19:08.166 --> 00:19:08.999
(Rich laughs)

424
00:19:08.999 --> 00:19:10.480
And like, so that's not happening.

425
00:19:10.480 --> 00:19:12.920
And we rented it to this really cool vinyl record store.

426
00:19:12.920 --> 00:19:16.780
So, it's just cool to be like looking down from my office

427
00:19:16.780 --> 00:19:20.797
and it's like books, music, people in this little town.

428
00:19:20.797 --> 00:19:25.797
And I think the nice part about having this stuff at scale

429
00:19:26.080 --> 00:19:29.613
is it subsidizes the cool physical stuff.

430
00:19:31.548 --> 00:19:34.470
And to be able to,

431
00:19:34.470 --> 00:19:36.730
it's also just been humbling to do something

432
00:19:36.730 --> 00:19:40.330
that's like smaller and less lucrative.

433
00:19:40.330 --> 00:19:41.163
You know what I mean?

434
00:19:41.163 --> 00:19:42.300
Like I know that sounds weird,

435
00:19:42.300 --> 00:19:45.510
but like, to be like, "Oh, hey, it made $500 today.

436
00:19:45.510 --> 00:19:46.343
That's awesome."

437
00:19:46.343 --> 00:19:47.176
Right.

438
00:19:47.176 --> 00:19:48.210
It's crazy because like for no capital,

439
00:19:48.210 --> 00:19:50.370
you can put content up on the internet

440
00:19:51.363 --> 00:19:53.030
and reap financial reward.

441
00:19:53.030 --> 00:19:55.620
And this requires a tremendous amount of capital

442
00:19:55.620 --> 00:19:58.446
with almost no remuneration.

443
00:19:58.446 --> 00:19:59.910
Yes.

444
00:19:59.910 --> 00:20:01.453
It's symbolic, I think.

445
00:20:01.453 --> 00:20:03.330
And it's consistent with who you are.

446
00:20:03.330 --> 00:20:04.163
But it's cool.

447
00:20:04.163 --> 00:20:05.217
Like people will come in and they'd be like,

448
00:20:05.217 --> 00:20:07.016
"Oh, what should I read?"

449
00:20:07.016 --> 00:20:08.440
And I'm like, you should read Rich Roll's book,

450
00:20:08.440 --> 00:20:11.770
like to physically be able to be like this book.

451
00:20:11.770 --> 00:20:13.090
And like they wouldn't have been able

452
00:20:13.090 --> 00:20:15.074
to discover it otherwise.

453
00:20:15.074 --> 00:20:16.870
And then they like they take it home.

454
00:20:16.870 --> 00:20:17.810
Like there is something cool.

455
00:20:17.810 --> 00:20:21.010
Quentin Tarantino and the video store in Manhattan Beach

456
00:20:21.010 --> 00:20:22.200
recommending movies.

457
00:20:22.200 --> 00:20:25.490
Yeah, and again, like the math is more like

458
00:20:25.490 --> 00:20:28.610
does it not lose a lot of money?

459
00:20:28.610 --> 00:20:30.550
That like success is just different.

460
00:20:30.550 --> 00:20:33.450
It's not like how many millions of people watched this

461
00:20:33.450 --> 00:20:34.300
or came into this?

462
00:20:34.300 --> 00:20:39.300
It's like, is it supporting itself?

463
00:20:39.540 --> 00:20:40.373
Right?

464
00:20:40.373 --> 00:20:42.050
Because and it's subsidized by other stuff.

465
00:20:42.050 --> 00:20:47.050
But like, is it, and are you having fun doing it?

466
00:20:47.650 --> 00:20:50.170
Do you find that people drop in

467
00:20:50.170 --> 00:20:51.500
just because they wanna meet you

468
00:20:51.500 --> 00:20:53.010
and you're upstairs trying to write

469
00:20:53.010 --> 00:20:54.433
and you have to contend with that.

470
00:20:54.433 --> 00:20:55.266
There's a little of that.

471
00:20:55.266 --> 00:20:58.410
I mean, the best part about it was like for the,

472
00:20:58.410 --> 00:21:00.380
we basically, we were starting in January

473
00:21:00.380 --> 00:21:02.320
and we really didn't open until late January,

474
00:21:02.320 --> 00:21:03.190
the following year,

475
00:21:03.190 --> 00:21:05.580
because it just didn't make sense and we didn't need to.

476
00:21:05.580 --> 00:21:07.870
So, I just felt from a COVID perspective,

477
00:21:07.870 --> 00:21:10.293
like I don't need to force this to happen.

478
00:21:11.280 --> 00:21:14.430
So, like it was magical

479
00:21:14.430 --> 00:21:16.940
to have like 5,000 square feet to myself

480
00:21:16.940 --> 00:21:21.550
as I worked on the new book in this enormous bubble

481
00:21:21.550 --> 00:21:23.173
for a year.

482
00:21:24.720 --> 00:21:26.240
But yeah, people do come in

483
00:21:26.240 --> 00:21:28.840
and there are certain days or times

484
00:21:28.840 --> 00:21:30.790
where I'm like I'm up for that.

485
00:21:30.790 --> 00:21:33.390
And then it also forces me

486
00:21:33.390 --> 00:21:34.850
to practice the discipline a little bit,

487
00:21:34.850 --> 00:21:38.430
because it's like, oh yeah, I could come down and say hi.

488
00:21:38.430 --> 00:21:41.470
But if I did that 50 times a day, I would get nothing done.

489
00:21:41.470 --> 00:21:43.492
And so, I have to be like, no, I'm like,

490
00:21:43.492 --> 00:21:45.540
"I would like to, but I can't."

491
00:21:45.540 --> 00:21:47.460
I think that's something,

492
00:21:47.460 --> 00:21:50.823
as we do transition to whatever the new normal is,

493
00:21:51.999 --> 00:21:53.730
it's like, I really got,

494
00:21:53.730 --> 00:21:57.500
I really benefited from the can't of the last year

495
00:21:57.500 --> 00:22:02.500
and a half, or like shouldn't to the, I could,

496
00:22:03.040 --> 00:22:04.870
but I'm not going to.

497
00:22:04.870 --> 00:22:08.190
How does that frame how you're making decisions

498
00:22:08.190 --> 00:22:10.230
now that the aperture of the world

499
00:22:10.230 --> 00:22:11.300
is opening up a little bit?

500
00:22:11.300 --> 00:22:12.730
I mean, obviously, you have a book coming out.

501
00:22:12.730 --> 00:22:14.420
So you're putting yourself out there

502
00:22:14.420 --> 00:22:16.186
and you're gonna do a bunch of stuff.

503
00:22:16.186 --> 00:22:21.186
But in general, that calculus of saying yes or saying no,

504
00:22:21.540 --> 00:22:25.750
has the past year changed how you think about that?

505
00:22:25.750 --> 00:22:28.760
Well, it was a very vivid illustration

506
00:22:28.760 --> 00:22:30.630
of opportunity costs.

507
00:22:30.630 --> 00:22:34.580
So, like I, for instance, was under the impression

508
00:22:34.580 --> 00:22:36.913
I was pretty productive on the road.

509
00:22:37.750 --> 00:22:41.840
But to then not be on the road, now I have,

510
00:22:41.840 --> 00:22:44.220
well, I just didn't have a control variable.

511
00:22:44.220 --> 00:22:47.280
I didn't really ever have evidence

512
00:22:47.280 --> 00:22:49.310
of like what the difference was,

513
00:22:49.310 --> 00:22:52.470
because I would never go long enough without traveling.

514
00:22:52.470 --> 00:22:53.670
Like I don't know about you,

515
00:22:53.670 --> 00:22:58.360
but like I'd never gone in my life 18 months

516
00:22:58.360 --> 00:22:59.480
without getting on an airplane.

517
00:22:59.480 --> 00:23:01.040
Well, part of the whole reason

518
00:23:01.040 --> 00:23:03.050
for living in the part of the world that you live in

519
00:23:03.050 --> 00:23:05.760
is its accessibility to both coasts.

520
00:23:05.760 --> 00:23:07.120
Yes.

521
00:23:07.120 --> 00:23:08.630
Although also I live in the country

522
00:23:08.630 --> 00:23:10.570
because I like living in the country, right?

523
00:23:10.570 --> 00:23:14.460
And so, yet I was like very rarely there.

524
00:23:14.460 --> 00:23:17.450
And so, to be able to write a book

525
00:23:17.450 --> 00:23:19.800
where I did no travel,

526
00:23:19.800 --> 00:23:23.280
it was like not only did I think it's better,

527
00:23:23.280 --> 00:23:25.500
but it was easier and faster.

528
00:23:25.500 --> 00:23:30.500
So, this was the like this book was the least painful

529
00:23:30.794 --> 00:23:32.563
of all my books.

530
00:23:33.931 --> 00:23:34.764
And it-

531
00:23:34.764 --> 00:23:36.220
Because of the lack of distractions.

532
00:23:36.220 --> 00:23:37.090
The lack of distraction.

533
00:23:37.090 --> 00:23:40.960
And so, I was working every single day, which is great.

534
00:23:40.960 --> 00:23:43.200
But like I could work,

535
00:23:43.200 --> 00:23:47.010
like I felt like I was,

536
00:23:47.010 --> 00:23:49.280
I don't wanna say I was working part-time, because I wasn't,

537
00:23:49.280 --> 00:23:51.990
but it was so much more manageable

538
00:23:51.990 --> 00:23:55.920
because I wasn't digging myself out of a hole constantly.

539
00:23:55.920 --> 00:23:58.730
I was never like playing catch up.

540
00:23:58.730 --> 00:24:01.440
And so, to have a really clear sense

541
00:24:01.440 --> 00:24:04.430
of like this is what this is costing me.

542
00:24:04.430 --> 00:24:06.211
Like every time you say yes to something,

543
00:24:06.211 --> 00:24:08.110
you're saying no to something else and vice versa.

544
00:24:08.110 --> 00:24:11.640
And so, if all becomes impossible to say yes

545
00:24:11.640 --> 00:24:12.720
to a bunch of stuff,

546
00:24:12.720 --> 00:24:16.060
you now have a much better sense of like all the things

547
00:24:16.060 --> 00:24:17.843
that you were saying no to.

548
00:24:18.790 --> 00:24:19.623
Like I mean-

549
00:24:19.623 --> 00:24:22.010
Or the extent to which your mind will search

550
00:24:22.010 --> 00:24:24.880
for other distractions to fill the vacuum.

551
00:24:24.880 --> 00:24:26.161
And I'm pretty good about that.

552
00:24:26.161 --> 00:24:28.900
I'll start really doubling down on YouTube now.

553
00:24:28.900 --> 00:24:30.443
I'm gonna do a podcast.

554
00:24:31.440 --> 00:24:33.890
Although it's like, oh, okay, so you could travel

555
00:24:33.890 --> 00:24:35.810
or you could do a podcast from your house.

556
00:24:35.810 --> 00:24:40.810
What's a better sort of what's a less disruptive pursuit?

557
00:24:41.300 --> 00:24:45.390
And so, I think it was just very illustrative to me

558
00:24:45.390 --> 00:24:46.830
of what it was like.

559
00:24:46.830 --> 00:24:48.797
So, I flew like a couple of weeks ago,

560
00:24:48.797 --> 00:24:51.750
and I did do my first in-person talk.

561
00:24:51.750 --> 00:24:54.120
And like, I was trying to work on the plane,

562
00:24:54.120 --> 00:24:56.320
like I've always worked on planes.

563
00:24:56.320 --> 00:25:01.180
And I was like I couldn't think at all.

564
00:25:01.180 --> 00:25:05.820
And I was like, "Oh, I must have been pushing through this

565
00:25:05.820 --> 00:25:08.900
for the last 10 years of my career."

566
00:25:08.900 --> 00:25:11.060
So, it's like it's sort of like when,

567
00:25:11.060 --> 00:25:11.893
and I'm sure you-

568
00:25:11.893 --> 00:25:13.260
But also working on a plane

569
00:25:13.260 --> 00:25:15.020
was probably more of a necessity

570
00:25:15.020 --> 00:25:18.950
because your your time was so much more precious

571
00:25:18.950 --> 00:25:20.610
in comparison to what it is now.

572
00:25:20.610 --> 00:25:22.160
Yeah, but and you of all people know this,

573
00:25:22.160 --> 00:25:24.740
like when you cut something out of your diet

574
00:25:24.740 --> 00:25:26.810
and then you add it back into your diet

575
00:25:26.810 --> 00:25:29.357
and you feel disgusting and you're like,

576
00:25:29.357 --> 00:25:32.150
"Oh, but I was eating this every day."

577
00:25:32.150 --> 00:25:34.440
You're like, so it's not new

578
00:25:34.440 --> 00:25:36.300
that it's making me feel disgusting,

579
00:25:36.300 --> 00:25:39.280
it's that I had normalized that feeling.

580
00:25:39.280 --> 00:25:40.113
Yeah.

581
00:25:40.113 --> 00:25:43.000
And so, to take a whole bunch of stuff away,

582
00:25:43.000 --> 00:25:44.360
then put it back in and you're like,

583
00:25:44.360 --> 00:25:49.360
oh, I was like operating through a fog

584
00:25:49.530 --> 00:25:52.890
that clearly had real impact.

585
00:25:52.890 --> 00:25:56.460
And it's not that the work wasn't good.

586
00:25:56.460 --> 00:25:58.620
It's that I was having to work extra hard

587
00:25:58.620 --> 00:25:59.890
to get to that level.

588
00:25:59.890 --> 00:26:03.030
It's like sort of like you're operating with a headwind

589
00:26:03.030 --> 00:26:03.863
or something.

590
00:26:05.070 --> 00:26:06.987
Yeah, interesting.

591
00:26:06.987 --> 00:26:10.160
You are pretty conservative throughout this period.

592
00:26:10.160 --> 00:26:11.840
Like you really were at home.

593
00:26:11.840 --> 00:26:13.390
You weren't doing anything.

594
00:26:13.390 --> 00:26:15.080
Yeah, I mean, I think part of it

595
00:26:15.080 --> 00:26:20.080
was like just the decision to like it was working,

596
00:26:20.170 --> 00:26:23.290
so like why fix it if it's not broke?

597
00:26:23.290 --> 00:26:24.800
But I think a big part,

598
00:26:24.800 --> 00:26:27.500
I took it seriously in the sense of like,

599
00:26:27.500 --> 00:26:29.432
well, I can just do that.

600
00:26:29.432 --> 00:26:32.950
Like I don't have to send my kids to school.

601
00:26:32.950 --> 00:26:34.090
We can homeschool them.

602
00:26:34.090 --> 00:26:35.800
Or I don't have to travel.

603
00:26:35.800 --> 00:26:37.680
I can say no to things.

604
00:26:37.680 --> 00:26:40.850
And so, I did feel like there was some moral obligation.

605
00:26:40.850 --> 00:26:42.630
And I've been somewhat disappointed with people I know

606
00:26:42.630 --> 00:26:44.140
who didn't maybe agree with this,

607
00:26:44.140 --> 00:26:47.010
but I felt like there was some moral obligation

608
00:26:47.010 --> 00:26:51.200
of like if you can take weight off the system,

609
00:26:51.200 --> 00:26:52.923
like you should do it.

610
00:26:54.530 --> 00:26:55.910
And so, we did it.

611
00:26:55.910 --> 00:26:57.343
And like we because where we live

612
00:26:57.343 --> 00:26:58.920
and how our life is set up,

613
00:26:58.920 --> 00:27:01.060
it's like we had everything we needed.

614
00:27:01.060 --> 00:27:01.930
We had space.

615
00:27:01.930 --> 00:27:04.730
We went in a two-bedroom apartment in Brooklyn.

616
00:27:04.730 --> 00:27:08.810
And so, we were like let's just do this.

617
00:27:08.810 --> 00:27:10.400
There's something idyllic about it, too.

618
00:27:10.400 --> 00:27:11.610
As a young father,

619
00:27:11.610 --> 00:27:15.250
this opportunity to spend so much time with your kids

620
00:27:15.250 --> 00:27:18.900
in this bucolic kind of environment of,

621
00:27:18.900 --> 00:27:21.350
on some level, a working ranch, I guess.

622
00:27:21.350 --> 00:27:22.640
Like I've never visited,

623
00:27:22.640 --> 00:27:25.900
but you've got cattle, you've got some livestock,

624
00:27:25.900 --> 00:27:27.370
you've got a beautiful home

625
00:27:27.370 --> 00:27:30.770
and kind of tending to the land with your children,

626
00:27:30.770 --> 00:27:33.380
with your family, while also being a writer.

627
00:27:33.380 --> 00:27:35.630
Like that's kinda beautiful.

628
00:27:35.630 --> 00:27:36.850
It was amazing.

629
00:27:36.850 --> 00:27:38.691
I mean, very privileged, of course.

630
00:27:38.691 --> 00:27:40.440
But it was wonderful.

