00:32:25.060 --> 00:32:30.060
I spent a lot of time ruminating on kind of sense-making

736
00:32:30.730 --> 00:32:33.440
how to make sense of our time.

737
00:32:33.440 --> 00:32:36.710
This national divide that we find ourselves in,

738
00:32:36.710 --> 00:32:41.280
a moment in which there's this dispersion of news

739
00:32:41.280 --> 00:32:44.040
and information in a partizan silos.

740
00:32:44.040 --> 00:32:46.190
There's a breakdown from my perspective,

741
00:32:46.190 --> 00:32:48.310
in healthy communication.

742
00:32:48.310 --> 00:32:53.310
And we kinda lost our tether when it comes to rationality,

743
00:32:54.540 --> 00:32:57.170
appreciation for nuance.

744
00:32:57.170 --> 00:32:59.390
Our sort of respect for one another

745
00:32:59.390 --> 00:33:03.440
has been supplanted with binary tribalism.

746
00:33:03.440 --> 00:33:06.400
There's an erosion of trust in institutions.

747
00:33:06.400 --> 00:33:10.250
Meanwhile, this ascent in conspiratorial thinking,

748
00:33:10.250 --> 00:33:15.250
a dearth of leadership where tribalism prevails over virtue,

749
00:33:15.350 --> 00:33:16.980
which we're gonna talk about.

750
00:33:16.980 --> 00:33:19.010
And we're more interested in taking others down

751
00:33:19.010 --> 00:33:21.240
than rising ourselves up.

752
00:33:21.240 --> 00:33:23.290
And I wonder how much of that

753
00:33:23.290 --> 00:33:25.860
is rooted in this American idea

754
00:33:25.860 --> 00:33:28.830
of individualism over community

755
00:33:28.830 --> 00:33:33.073
seems to be a somewhat uniquely American ethos.

756
00:33:34.819 --> 00:33:37.697
And a lot of people who, I guess,

757
00:33:39.110 --> 00:33:42.600
technically can be considered to be behaving,

758
00:33:42.600 --> 00:33:45.190
quote unquote, courageously,

759
00:33:45.190 --> 00:33:48.980
but through the lens of their particular strain

760
00:33:48.980 --> 00:33:50.910
of perceived truth.

761
00:33:50.910 --> 00:33:53.210
Well, we talk a lot about freedom

762
00:33:53.210 --> 00:33:56.250
and not very much about responsibility.

763
00:33:56.250 --> 00:33:58.290
And I think especially in the American system,

764
00:33:58.290 --> 00:34:03.290
the whole point of a system that gives

765
00:34:03.350 --> 00:34:07.373
or allows for a lot of personal liberty,

766
00:34:08.340 --> 00:34:13.340
was intended to be checked by private virtue.

767
00:34:14.820 --> 00:34:19.820
So, just because you can, doesn't mean that you should.

768
00:34:19.970 --> 00:34:22.000
Or just because it's legal,

769
00:34:22.000 --> 00:34:24.620
doesn't mean you should let yourself do it.

770
00:34:24.620 --> 00:34:29.420
And so, I think we are really struggling as a society

771
00:34:29.420 --> 00:34:33.130
and as individuals to wrap our heads around,

772
00:34:33.130 --> 00:34:38.130
like where sort of our freedom ends and our responsibility

773
00:34:38.320 --> 00:34:41.050
or obligations to other people begins.

774
00:34:41.050 --> 00:34:42.570
And that's sort of what I was talking about,

775
00:34:42.570 --> 00:34:46.160
where it's like, look, I'm young, I'm healthy,

776
00:34:46.160 --> 00:34:48.910
now I'm vaccinated, I could do whatever I want

777
00:34:50.170 --> 00:34:52.420
or I could have done whatever I wanted despite those things.

778
00:34:52.420 --> 00:34:56.775
But like this isn't I could make a lot of decisions

779
00:34:56.775 --> 00:35:00.290
and we all can, not just related to the pandemic,

780
00:35:00.290 --> 00:35:05.080
where the primary recipient

781
00:35:05.080 --> 00:35:09.850
of the consequences of those decisions is not borne by you.

782
00:35:09.850 --> 00:35:13.790
But so it takes some self-awareness and self-control

783
00:35:13.790 --> 00:35:17.080
and also courage to be like, I could do that,

784
00:35:17.080 --> 00:35:19.970
but that's not a good way to live.

785
00:35:19.970 --> 00:35:22.340
And that's not a fair way to live.

786
00:35:22.340 --> 00:35:24.820
And so, I think we're struggling

787
00:35:24.820 --> 00:35:29.820
with like sort of what our obligations are to each other.

788
00:35:30.563 --> 00:35:32.270
Like so people would say with the bookstore,

789
00:35:32.270 --> 00:35:36.190
they'll be like, "Oh, must be great being in Texas,

790
00:35:36.190 --> 00:35:38.910
very sort of conducive to like private."

791
00:35:38.910 --> 00:35:42.420
Like Texas has been like do whatever you want

792
00:35:42.420 --> 00:35:44.180
as a business, right?

793
00:35:44.180 --> 00:35:47.353
Except for some very hypocritical things

794
00:35:47.353 --> 00:35:49.010
that they limited businesses.

795
00:35:49.010 --> 00:35:52.530
But anyway, it's like we could have opened.

796
00:35:52.530 --> 00:35:55.270
We didn't have to have a mask mandate.

797
00:35:55.270 --> 00:35:57.850
We could have done a lot of things.

798
00:35:57.850 --> 00:36:02.730
But I actually don't find that to be a gift.

799
00:36:02.730 --> 00:36:07.080
I find it to be somebody in elected leadership

800
00:36:07.080 --> 00:36:09.500
passing the buck to somebody else.

801
00:36:09.500 --> 00:36:12.617
Because somebody ultimately has to decide,

802
00:36:12.617 --> 00:36:16.210
"Hey, am I gonna be part of the problem,

803
00:36:16.210 --> 00:36:17.400
or am I gonna be part of the solution?"

804
00:36:17.400 --> 00:36:19.040
So, I think that's really where our struggle is.

805
00:36:19.040 --> 00:36:22.660
It's just like here's what I'm allowed to do,

806
00:36:22.660 --> 00:36:27.260
but here's what I allow myself to do per my conscience

807
00:36:27.260 --> 00:36:30.310
and my sense of duty and obligations.

808
00:36:30.310 --> 00:36:31.143
Right.

809
00:36:31.143 --> 00:36:33.420
I think you really nailed it with this idea

810
00:36:33.420 --> 00:36:36.293
that all of the focus is on freedom,

811
00:36:38.370 --> 00:36:41.360
when in fact, freedom needs to be checked

812
00:36:41.360 --> 00:36:42.740
by responsibility, right?

813
00:36:42.740 --> 00:36:43.790
Like we're not talking

814
00:36:43.790 --> 00:36:47.980
about what our collective responsibility is to each other,

815
00:36:47.980 --> 00:36:50.600
and we're primarily focused on what we can do

816
00:36:50.600 --> 00:36:55.200
or what my rights are and expressing however I feel

817
00:36:55.200 --> 00:36:56.170
I want to be.

818
00:36:56.170 --> 00:37:00.140
And certainly there can be virtue in that.

819
00:37:00.140 --> 00:37:04.260
And there's something to be said for understanding

820
00:37:04.260 --> 00:37:07.820
that being part of this culture in America

821
00:37:07.820 --> 00:37:10.430
is the opportunity to be free.

822
00:37:10.430 --> 00:37:13.430
But we all need to shoulder our collective responsibility

823
00:37:13.430 --> 00:37:14.411
to each other.

824
00:37:14.411 --> 00:37:17.670
And I feel like we've lost that sense of community.

825
00:37:17.670 --> 00:37:19.700
And we see-

826
00:37:19.700 --> 00:37:21.670
We have a we have a particular responsibility

827
00:37:21.670 --> 00:37:26.670
that Stoics and say to the vulnerable and to the less-abled

828
00:37:28.110 --> 00:37:33.110
and to the people who can't look out for themselves.

829
00:37:35.300 --> 00:37:40.300
So, like a friend of ours' father who was vaccinated

830
00:37:41.070 --> 00:37:44.500
but was also a cancer patient, just died of COVID.

831
00:37:44.500 --> 00:37:46.470
Like where's that guy's freedom?

832
00:37:46.470 --> 00:37:47.303
Right?

833
00:37:47.303 --> 00:37:49.400
Somebody took that freedom away from him

834
00:37:50.690 --> 00:37:55.210
by their choice to not take a thing seriously, right?

835
00:37:55.210 --> 00:37:59.380
Or to not think about the consequences of their actions.

836
00:37:59.380 --> 00:38:02.210
And it's the person who touched the person,

837
00:38:02.210 --> 00:38:03.380
who touched the person, who touched the person,

838
00:38:03.380 --> 00:38:05.320
but the point is really stopping

839
00:38:05.320 --> 00:38:10.320
and thinking like what am I contributing

840
00:38:11.010 --> 00:38:14.860
or taking away from what the Stoics call the common good.

841
00:38:14.860 --> 00:38:16.350
Marcus Aurelius, the Emperor,

842
00:38:16.350 --> 00:38:18.410
the most powerful man in the world.

843
00:38:18.410 --> 00:38:20.344
Not that Rome was a particularly wonderful place,

844
00:38:20.344 --> 00:38:23.410
but he refers to this idea of the common good,

845
00:38:23.410 --> 00:38:26.430
like 50 or 60 times in meditation.

846
00:38:26.430 --> 00:38:29.140
It's like the thing he's constantly thinking about

847
00:38:29.140 --> 00:38:30.740
is sort of where,

848
00:38:30.740 --> 00:38:32.970
and he's thinking about this during a pandemic.

849
00:38:32.970 --> 00:38:36.040
Like what are the choices or actions I'm taking

850
00:38:36.040 --> 00:38:39.550
and how are they impacting the people around me?

851
00:38:39.550 --> 00:38:44.550
The Stoics have this idea they call the circles of concern.

852
00:38:44.750 --> 00:38:47.623
So, we have like first off, yourself,

853
00:38:47.623 --> 00:38:49.810
then you have your immediate family,

854
00:38:49.810 --> 00:38:51.640
then you have like your community.

855
00:38:51.640 --> 00:38:53.060
And it gets bigger and bigger and bigger

856
00:38:53.060 --> 00:38:55.230
until it's everyone in the world.

857
00:38:55.230 --> 00:38:56.330
But the whole idea was like,

858
00:38:56.330 --> 00:39:00.970
how do you take the people on the far this ring

859
00:39:00.970 --> 00:39:03.900
and bring them closer to the center?

860
00:39:03.900 --> 00:39:05.950
Or how do you take the feelings

861
00:39:05.950 --> 00:39:07.290
that you have towards the people

862
00:39:07.290 --> 00:39:10.120
that you are biologically related to

863
00:39:10.120 --> 00:39:14.000
or genuinely sort of genuinely care about,

864
00:39:14.000 --> 00:39:16.330
how do you kind of radiate that outwards

865
00:39:16.330 --> 00:39:17.800
to as many people as possible?

866
00:39:17.800 --> 00:39:20.850
So, that's, I think, it's really easy to be selfish.

867
00:39:20.850 --> 00:39:23.460
It's really easy to just think about all the things

868
00:39:23.460 --> 00:39:26.450
you have going on and how things affect you.

869
00:39:26.450 --> 00:39:28.620
And I think one of the things we really spent some time

870
00:39:28.620 --> 00:39:30.047
thinking about was like,

871
00:39:30.047 --> 00:39:31.710
and it's hard.

872
00:39:31.710 --> 00:39:34.680
It was particularly hard not to do that

873
00:39:34.680 --> 00:39:36.360
when we watch the sort of racial reckoning

874
00:39:36.360 --> 00:39:40.310
that we went through as a country in the summer of 2020

875
00:39:40.310 --> 00:39:43.120
to be like, oh, there's a lot of things

876
00:39:43.120 --> 00:39:45.120
that we haven't been thinking about

877
00:39:45.120 --> 00:39:47.680
or people that we haven't been caring about

878
00:39:47.680 --> 00:39:52.680
or just problems that haven't affected me

879
00:39:53.180 --> 00:39:54.650
that I don't think about.

880
00:39:54.650 --> 00:39:56.810
But by not thinking about,

881
00:39:56.810 --> 00:40:01.513
I am complicit in their continuation.

882
00:40:03.150 --> 00:40:08.150
Yeah, and I suppose the more power that you accumulate,

883
00:40:09.150 --> 00:40:14.150
the more ability you have and responsibility that you have

884
00:40:14.760 --> 00:40:17.160
to impact those outer rings, right?

885
00:40:17.160 --> 00:40:20.100
Like when you're lacking power,

886
00:40:20.100 --> 00:40:23.900
your influence extends not very far to just yourself

887
00:40:23.900 --> 00:40:25.600
and perhaps your family, et cetera.

888
00:40:25.600 --> 00:40:29.150
But Marcus Aurelius being an extreme example.

889
00:40:29.150 --> 00:40:32.280
But anyone who accumulates some level of power,

890
00:40:32.280 --> 00:40:34.460
I think the message is that they need

891
00:40:34.460 --> 00:40:35.990
to shoulder that responsibility

892
00:40:35.990 --> 00:40:37.220
and think more profoundly

893
00:40:37.220 --> 00:40:39.319
about the impact of their decisions

894
00:40:39.319 --> 00:40:44.313
as the kind of Venn diagram or these concentric circles.

895
00:40:45.330 --> 00:40:46.423
Well, have you noticed or thought

896
00:40:46.423 --> 00:40:49.860
like it's been weird to me to watch people I know

897
00:40:49.860 --> 00:40:52.570
with very large platforms

898
00:40:52.570 --> 00:40:55.563
just sort of sit a lot of things out.

899
00:40:56.410 --> 00:40:57.243
You know what I mean?

900
00:40:57.243 --> 00:40:58.960
And there's a balance, of course,

901
00:40:58.960 --> 00:41:02.960
because if your platform is based on X

902
00:41:02.960 --> 00:41:04.700
and you're talking about Y,

903
00:41:04.700 --> 00:41:08.300
you can lose the reason that people follow you, right?

904
00:41:08.300 --> 00:41:12.640
And there's something to be said about being a safe place

905
00:41:12.640 --> 00:41:13.870
where everyone can come together

906
00:41:13.870 --> 00:41:15.490
and not have to think about certain things.

907
00:41:15.490 --> 00:41:16.430
I totally get it.

908
00:41:16.430 --> 00:41:19.410
But it is interesting to me to watch the way,

909
00:41:19.410 --> 00:41:20.790
like certain pet issues,

910
00:41:20.790 --> 00:41:22.490
people feel very emphatic

911
00:41:22.490 --> 00:41:24.250
and be happy to use their platform

912
00:41:24.250 --> 00:41:26.460
to talk about over and over and over again,

913
00:41:26.460 --> 00:41:30.480
which might have a minuscule impact on humanity.

914
00:41:30.480 --> 00:41:32.250
But then they don't wanna talk about this

915
00:41:32.250 --> 00:41:35.782
because they know it will upset some certain vocal minority.

916
00:41:35.782 --> 00:41:36.615
Sure, it's complicated.

917
00:41:36.615 --> 00:41:37.660
Look, it's it's hard.

918
00:41:37.660 --> 00:41:39.140
And we are in an environment

919
00:41:39.140 --> 00:41:41.630
where if you say the wrong thing,

920
00:41:41.630 --> 00:41:43.630
the repercussions are very real.

921
00:41:43.630 --> 00:41:46.000
So, I wanna be sensitive to that.

922
00:41:46.000 --> 00:41:47.790
But I agree and I understand that.

923
00:41:47.790 --> 00:41:52.680
Like it is for a lot of people, a high-wire act.

924
00:41:52.680 --> 00:41:54.390
Like they don't wanna get canceled

925
00:41:54.390 --> 00:41:56.123
or they're afraid of,

926
00:41:58.324 --> 00:41:59.360
and this goes into courage also,

927
00:41:59.360 --> 00:42:03.570
like fear of others and opinions of other people

928
00:42:03.570 --> 00:42:05.950
and being criticized, et cetera.

929
00:42:05.950 --> 00:42:07.513
I mean, that was a reason why,

930
00:42:08.950 --> 00:42:11.493
I went through a little bit of that myself,

931
00:42:12.330 --> 00:42:14.930
and that was the reason why we did a pivot with the podcast.

932
00:42:14.930 --> 00:42:17.010
And we kind of do this different kind of show

933
00:42:17.010 --> 00:42:17.843
every two weeks

934
00:42:17.843 --> 00:42:20.100
where we talk about more contemporaneous events.

935
00:42:20.100 --> 00:42:24.470
Because it did feel weird and not right

936
00:42:24.470 --> 00:42:28.930
to just do an episode about the microbiome

937
00:42:28.930 --> 00:42:33.640
when like Minneapolis is about to burn to the ground.

938
00:42:33.640 --> 00:42:35.980
I just couldn't sleep with myself.

939
00:42:35.980 --> 00:42:38.900
So, I felt compelled to address these things.

940
00:42:38.900 --> 00:42:40.373
I felt the same thing with Stoicism.

941
00:42:40.373 --> 00:42:42.770
But I'm also compassionate to people who are afraid

942
00:42:42.770 --> 00:42:44.420
to speak their mind right now,

943
00:42:44.420 --> 00:42:45.850
because it is a culture

944
00:42:45.850 --> 00:42:49.030
in which we're not very forgiving of people's traggressions.

945
00:42:49.030 --> 00:42:50.260
That's true.

946
00:42:50.260 --> 00:42:54.510
I mean, I felt like with Stoicism, there's a whole bunch

947
00:42:54.510 --> 00:42:59.030
of sort of self-improvement elements to it right?

948
00:42:59.030 --> 00:43:04.030
Resiliency, sort of productivity, controlling your emotions.

949
00:43:04.400 --> 00:43:06.360
And I could spend my whole life

950
00:43:06.360 --> 00:43:08.400
just talking about those things.

951
00:43:08.400 --> 00:43:11.570
And those things are much less controversial

952
00:43:11.570 --> 00:43:13.360
than the other things.

953
00:43:13.360 --> 00:43:15.550
And I remember just thinking

954
00:43:15.550 --> 00:43:20.550
like what good is having the platform

955
00:43:21.800 --> 00:43:23.470
if you're only gonna use it

956
00:43:23.470 --> 00:43:25.723
to tell people what they wanna hear?

957
00:43:26.670 --> 00:43:28.320
There's a story about Lyndon Johnson

958
00:43:28.320 --> 00:43:29.310
after he becomes president.

959
00:43:29.310 --> 00:43:31.850
This thing he worked for like his whole life, right?

960
00:43:31.850 --> 00:43:36.410
Nobody like moved up the levels of power

961
00:43:36.410 --> 00:43:40.680
sort of more like slowly than Lyndon Johnson, right?

962
00:43:40.680 --> 00:43:43.230
Like he holds like every consecutive office

963
00:43:43.230 --> 00:43:44.600
on his way there.

964
00:43:44.600 --> 00:43:46.450
And he spends years as a politician

965
00:43:46.450 --> 00:43:49.130
before he eventually becomes president.

966
00:43:49.130 --> 00:43:53.160
And suddenly, there's this opportunity to pass civil rights.

967
00:43:53.160 --> 00:43:55.167
And one of his advisers says like,

968
00:43:55.167 --> 00:43:56.980
"Well, we should wait till you're reelected

969
00:43:56.980 --> 00:43:58.930
or like there's gonna be negative

970
00:43:58.930 --> 00:43:59.980
for the following reasons."

971
00:43:59.980 --> 00:44:01.607
And he says something like,

972
00:44:01.607 --> 00:44:03.942
"But what the hell is the presidency for?"

973
00:44:03.942 --> 00:44:07.490
Meaning, like we tell ourselves,

974
00:44:07.490 --> 00:44:09.150
like, hey, when I have X,

975
00:44:09.150 --> 00:44:12.080
then I'm gonna like use it for good.

976
00:44:12.080 --> 00:44:13.330
But then we never do it,

977
00:44:13.330 --> 00:44:16.460
because then we're like thinking about the next thing.

978
00:44:16.460 --> 00:44:19.321
And so, it's definitely something I've thought about.

979
00:44:19.321 --> 00:44:20.320
And it pretends to courage, too.

980
00:44:20.320 --> 00:44:24.800
But just ultimately, like what good is the success

981
00:44:24.800 --> 00:44:28.140
if it then actually makes you more conservative?

982
00:44:28.140 --> 00:44:29.310
And I don't mean that politically.

983
00:44:29.310 --> 00:44:32.540
I mean that in the sense of like you're now more risk averse

984
00:44:32.540 --> 00:44:34.440
because you don't wanna lose what you have.

985
00:44:34.440 --> 00:44:35.730
Yeah, well, I've noticed

986
00:44:35.730 --> 00:44:37.620
that you're pretty strident about this.

987
00:44:37.620 --> 00:44:41.020
Like you'll say something on social media

988
00:44:41.020 --> 00:44:43.517
and there'll be a litany of comments saying,

989
00:44:43.517 --> 00:44:45.380
"I can't believe you said that.

990
00:44:45.380 --> 00:44:47.150
Stick to Stoicism."

991
00:44:47.150 --> 00:44:49.616
Or, "How very anti-stoic of you."

992
00:44:49.616 --> 00:44:53.210
And then you get in and like you drag these people.

993
00:44:53.210 --> 00:44:54.150
Like-

994
00:44:54.150 --> 00:44:55.523
I've tried to actually stop doing that

995
00:44:55.523 --> 00:44:57.660
because it was making me unhappy.

996
00:44:57.660 --> 00:44:59.610
Yeah, I was like that part of it,

997
00:44:59.610 --> 00:45:01.960
I'm not sure is in your best interest.

998
00:45:01.960 --> 00:45:03.920
But I have noticed,

999
00:45:03.920 --> 00:45:07.230
and this is a good segue into this broader conversation

1000
00:45:07.230 --> 00:45:10.280
about courage, like to have the courage of your convictions,

1001
00:45:10.280 --> 00:45:13.400
understanding and knowing beforehand,

1002
00:45:13.400 --> 00:45:14.360
if you say this,

1003
00:45:14.360 --> 00:45:16.920
it is gonna provoke a certain kind of reaction

1004
00:45:16.920 --> 00:45:18.920
that is gonna have ramifications

1005
00:45:18.920 --> 00:45:21.420
in terms of the number of people who are gonna follow you

1006
00:45:21.420 --> 00:45:22.890
or buy your books or whatnot.

1007
00:45:22.890 --> 00:45:25.220
Yeah, it it's sort of, again,

1008
00:45:25.220 --> 00:45:29.563
it's like what good is the success if you,

1009
00:45:30.520 --> 00:45:34.540
it's like that line in the first season of "Billions,"

1010
00:45:34.540 --> 00:45:36.250
which says like, "What good is fuck you money,

1011
00:45:36.250 --> 00:45:37.083
if you never say fuck you?"

1012
00:45:37.083 --> 00:45:38.690
It's so funny that you say that,

1013
00:45:38.690 --> 00:45:43.480
because I just rewatched the pilot of "Billions" last night.

1014
00:45:43.480 --> 00:45:44.313
And so, I was thinking,

1015
00:45:44.313 --> 00:45:46.730
when you were saying that, I was seeing Axe

1016
00:45:46.730 --> 00:45:48.610
saying that very line.

1017
00:45:48.610 --> 00:45:50.900
And I mean, he's using it to like basically enact

1018
00:45:50.900 --> 00:45:53.780
like to pursue like a personal grudge.

1019
00:45:53.780 --> 00:45:55.099
So, that's not what we're talking about.

1020
00:45:55.099 --> 00:45:55.932
But he-

1021
00:45:55.932 --> 00:45:59.190
And he's actually, he's operating at his peril

1022
00:45:59.190 --> 00:46:00.023
in that moment.

1023
00:46:00.023 --> 00:46:00.856
Yes. Yes.

1024
00:46:00.856 --> 00:46:02.967
But generally, we tell ourselves, like,

1025
00:46:02.967 --> 00:46:04.360
"Hey, when I have this."

1026
00:46:04.360 --> 00:46:05.510
And I've noticed this.

1027
00:46:05.510 --> 00:46:07.900
I've gotten to go to Washington a number of times

1028
00:46:07.900 --> 00:46:09.390
and talked to people who would read the books.

1029
00:46:09.390 --> 00:46:14.190
And like you think that this senator is powerful

1030
00:46:14.190 --> 00:46:16.410
because like they're one of 100 people.

1031
00:46:16.410 --> 00:46:19.413
And actually they don't see themselves as powerful

1032
00:46:19.413 --> 00:46:21.860
because they see this as a way station

1033
00:46:21.860 --> 00:46:24.660
on the way to another thing.

1034
00:46:24.660 --> 00:46:27.500
And then they say, when I get there,

1035
00:46:27.500 --> 00:46:28.907
then I'll open it up, right?

1036
00:46:28.907 --> 00:46:31.447
But of course, you never arrive at that place.

1037
00:46:31.447 --> 00:46:32.290
You never do.

1038
00:46:32.290 --> 00:46:33.123
It's insidious.

1039
00:46:33.123 --> 00:46:36.080
It's in the same way that you never feel like comfortable

1040
00:46:36.080 --> 00:46:36.913
or secure.

1041
00:46:36.913 --> 00:46:37.746
You never go like,

1042
00:46:37.746 --> 00:46:41.760
"Now I'm willing to like do the unpopular,

1043
00:46:41.760 --> 00:46:42.593
but correct thing."

1044
00:46:42.593 --> 00:46:43.460
Right.

1045
00:46:43.460 --> 00:46:48.460
There's many examples in the new book that reference that.

1046
00:46:48.600 --> 00:46:52.930
The one that comes to mind is Nixon versus Kennedy

