00:46:52.930 --> 00:46:57.083
in terms of which one of those guys decides to get involved

1048
00:46:57.083 --> 00:47:01.300
in getting Dr. King out of out of jail.

1049
00:47:01.300 --> 00:47:02.860
Nixon, I didn't realize

1050
00:47:02.860 --> 00:47:06.330
that Nixon had this pretty robust relationship with King.

1051
00:47:06.330 --> 00:47:07.163
They were friends.

1052
00:47:07.163 --> 00:47:07.996
They were buddies.

1053
00:47:07.996 --> 00:47:09.769
And he would call on him for advice.

1054
00:47:09.769 --> 00:47:13.240
And yet when the moment came for him to act,

1055
00:47:13.240 --> 00:47:14.200
he was reluctant

1056
00:47:14.200 --> 00:47:17.130
because he felt he would alienate the South.

1057
00:47:17.130 --> 00:47:19.720
Kennedy enters and does the right thing

1058
00:47:19.720 --> 00:47:21.630
and ultimately wins the election

1059
00:47:21.630 --> 00:47:23.340
by like half a point, right?

1060
00:47:23.340 --> 00:47:27.080
Yes, almost entirely because of the black community.

1061
00:47:27.080 --> 00:47:30.867
And but you can imagine Nixon telling himself,

1062
00:47:30.867 --> 00:47:33.710
"I'll deal with this after I'm president."

1063
00:47:33.710 --> 00:47:34.543
Right?

1064
00:47:34.543 --> 00:47:36.410
He's like, "Just I don't wanna piss these people.

1065
00:47:36.410 --> 00:47:38.950
I don't want to piss off the South.

1066
00:47:38.950 --> 00:47:43.320
And so, I'm not gonna get involved, but I'll do it later."

1067
00:47:43.320 --> 00:47:47.640
The irony being, because he fails in this moment of courage,

1068
00:47:47.640 --> 00:47:49.050
he doesn't become president.

1069
00:47:49.050 --> 00:47:51.100
Yeah, and the point being that the thing

1070
00:47:51.100 --> 00:47:53.110
that seems like the conservative choice

1071
00:47:53.110 --> 00:47:55.570
ends up being the greater risk.

1072
00:47:55.570 --> 00:48:00.570
Yeah, and that we often understate the risk

1073
00:48:00.650 --> 00:48:02.850
of doing nothing, right?

1074
00:48:02.850 --> 00:48:04.870
Like Jeff Bezos talks about,

1075
00:48:04.870 --> 00:48:07.950
he says, "I don't do bet the company bets."

1076
00:48:07.950 --> 00:48:12.950
Meaning I don't act conservatively risk averse day-to-day,

1077
00:48:14.810 --> 00:48:19.280
then you find yourself behind or boring

1078
00:48:19.280 --> 00:48:21.198
or lacking in innovation,

1079
00:48:21.198 --> 00:48:25.610
and then you have to bet it all on some moonshot.

1080
00:48:25.610 --> 00:48:30.610
Not literally, but some crazy idea or risky venture, right?

1081
00:48:31.590 --> 00:48:34.160
If you're regularly innovating and taking risks

1082
00:48:34.160 --> 00:48:36.290
and being generally courageous in your life,

1083
00:48:36.290 --> 00:48:39.630
if it's a habit, then it's not as scary.

1084
00:48:39.630 --> 00:48:42.630
It's only when you have not gotten involved,

1085
00:48:42.630 --> 00:48:44.450
not gotten involved, not gotten involved,

1086
00:48:44.450 --> 00:48:48.070
that then to get involved is a huge risk.

1087
00:48:48.070 --> 00:48:48.903
Yeah.

1088
00:48:48.903 --> 00:48:51.710
This idea of courage is a habit.

1089
00:48:51.710 --> 00:48:54.830
And the kind of athletic analogy to that

1090
00:48:54.830 --> 00:48:56.920
is basically just being consistent.

1091
00:48:56.920 --> 00:48:59.010
Like showing up every day and putting in the training

1092
00:48:59.010 --> 00:49:01.040
and doing the hard work,

1093
00:49:01.040 --> 00:49:04.240
not waiting until you're really late in the game

1094
00:49:04.240 --> 00:49:06.670
and then having to go out and do a 20-mile run

1095
00:49:06.670 --> 00:49:08.510
when you're ill-prepared for it, right?

1096
00:49:08.510 --> 00:49:10.250
Like you're just gonna get injured.

1097
00:49:10.250 --> 00:49:11.720
I think about it just kind of staying

1098
00:49:11.720 --> 00:49:13.130
at your fighting weight, right?

1099
00:49:13.130 --> 00:49:16.930
Like my sort of routine for writing

1100
00:49:16.930 --> 00:49:18.430
is I'm just always writing.

1101
00:49:18.430 --> 00:49:21.537
I'm not writing and then I'm done

1102
00:49:21.537 --> 00:49:23.527
and then I go back to my normal life

1103
00:49:23.527 --> 00:49:28.130
and then I'm intimidated by like going back into-

1104
00:49:28.130 --> 00:49:30.500
That's really, I think, your superpower.

1105
00:49:30.500 --> 00:49:34.380
Your ability to remain so consistent in your writing.

1106
00:49:34.380 --> 00:49:35.213
Thank you.

1107
00:49:35.213 --> 00:49:37.930
I mean, it's either a superpower or an addiction

1108
00:49:37.930 --> 00:49:39.640
or probably a combination of the two.

1109
00:49:39.640 --> 00:49:43.110
But I do just generally try to always be doing it

1110
00:49:43.110 --> 00:49:45.410
so there isn't the sort of whiplash

1111
00:49:45.410 --> 00:49:47.860
of like on, off, on, off.

1112
00:49:47.860 --> 00:49:50.340
Right. That's painful.

1113
00:49:50.340 --> 00:49:53.880
Because if you think to,

1114
00:49:53.880 --> 00:49:55.760
there's a great expression I actually have a chapter about

1115
00:49:55.760 --> 00:49:57.149
in the book,

1116
00:49:57.149 --> 00:49:59.549
but like it's like the world is a narrow bridge.

1117
00:50:00.600 --> 00:50:02.710
The important thing is to not be afraid.

1118
00:50:02.710 --> 00:50:04.520
Meaning like if you're just walking,

1119
00:50:04.520 --> 00:50:07.200
you just walk across the narrow bridge.

1120
00:50:07.200 --> 00:50:08.380
Like you don't think about it.

1121
00:50:08.380 --> 00:50:09.900
You just have to do it.

1122
00:50:09.900 --> 00:50:13.760
If you're stopping resting and looking out over the side

1123
00:50:13.760 --> 00:50:18.760
and thinking about it too much, that's when you fall.

1124
00:50:19.040 --> 00:50:21.740
And so with books, it's like on this project,

1125
00:50:21.740 --> 00:50:22.770
I'm doing not just one book,

1126
00:50:22.770 --> 00:50:25.500
I'm doing four books in a four-book series.

1127
00:50:25.500 --> 00:50:27.560
I'm just not thinking about it too much.

1128
00:50:27.560 --> 00:50:31.190
Like, I'm just everyday showing up and chipping away at it.

1129
00:50:31.190 --> 00:50:33.670
And I know that stuff will come out

1130
00:50:33.670 --> 00:50:35.210
of the other side of that.

1131
00:50:35.210 --> 00:50:38.030
But if I'm like, okay, here's the schedule

1132
00:50:38.030 --> 00:50:39.966
and I have to do this and then this,

1133
00:50:39.966 --> 00:50:42.450
like that's when I'll start to get in my own head about it.

1134
00:50:42.450 --> 00:50:45.690
And also knowing what your next three books are gonna be.

1135
00:50:45.690 --> 00:50:46.710
It's a blessing and a curse.

1136
00:50:46.710 --> 00:50:47.930
And I would imagine,

1137
00:50:47.930 --> 00:50:52.170
because it's a series on these four virtues,

1138
00:50:52.170 --> 00:50:55.340
in the preparation or the research for,

1139
00:50:55.340 --> 00:50:57.710
let's say the next virtue book that you're doing,

1140
00:50:57.710 --> 00:50:59.810
you're gonna come across research

1141
00:50:59.810 --> 00:51:01.790
relevant to the third and the fourth book.

1142
00:51:01.790 --> 00:51:04.200
So, you're kind of are you writing kind of sort of

1143
00:51:04.200 --> 00:51:06.407
on some level, all four of them at the same time

1144
00:51:06.407 --> 00:51:07.810
in different stages.

1145
00:51:07.810 --> 00:51:10.330
I'm researching all four of them at the same time.

1146
00:51:10.330 --> 00:51:11.640
Actually the hardest part is,

1147
00:51:11.640 --> 00:51:14.410
so, "Obstacle," "Ego," and "Stillness,"

1148
00:51:14.410 --> 00:51:17.690
the first trilogy that I did, it was accidental.

1149
00:51:17.690 --> 00:51:20.900
Like I wrote a book, then independently came up with an idea

1150
00:51:20.900 --> 00:51:23.570
for a second book and then a third book.

1151
00:51:23.570 --> 00:51:24.403
So-

1152
00:51:24.403 --> 00:51:25.810
Now you're operating like Marvel.

1153
00:51:25.810 --> 00:51:28.560
It's the expanded Ryan Holiday universe.

1154
00:51:28.560 --> 00:51:30.470
Well, the tricky part is the metaverse

1155
00:51:30.470 --> 00:51:31.910
of ancient philosophy.

1156
00:51:31.910 --> 00:51:33.777
Now that I look at those three books,

1157
00:51:33.777 --> 00:51:37.520
there's chapters I would like to trade in the books.

1158
00:51:37.520 --> 00:51:40.380
Like I'd like to move a chapter from "Ego" to "Stillness"

1159
00:51:40.380 --> 00:51:41.540
and vice versa,

1160
00:51:41.540 --> 00:51:43.800
or there's maybe chapters that shouldn't be

1161
00:51:43.800 --> 00:51:45.450
in either the three books that would be better

1162
00:51:45.450 --> 00:51:47.150
in this new series.

1163
00:51:47.150 --> 00:51:49.450
So, like as I was working on "Courage,"

1164
00:51:49.450 --> 00:51:51.470
it was the first time where I had to go,

1165
00:51:51.470 --> 00:51:52.720
here's something that's important to me

1166
00:51:52.720 --> 00:51:54.500
to say I wanna say it,

1167
00:51:54.500 --> 00:51:59.270
but do I have the restraint to not say it in this book

1168
00:51:59.270 --> 00:52:01.220
and leave it for this book?

1169
00:52:01.220 --> 00:52:04.470
And I've had up until all the way through galleys,

1170
00:52:04.470 --> 00:52:07.370
I was moving chapters around and cutting them and stuff.

1171
00:52:07.370 --> 00:52:11.520
So, it's harder in the sense that I'm having to think

1172
00:52:11.520 --> 00:52:15.300
in like four projects simultaneously.

1173
00:52:15.300 --> 00:52:18.650
And I can't not be thinking ahead,

1174
00:52:18.650 --> 00:52:21.160
because I might put something in one book

1175
00:52:21.160 --> 00:52:23.180
that then makes it impossible

1176
00:52:23.180 --> 00:52:25.410
or either contradicts what I would say in another one

1177
00:52:25.410 --> 00:52:29.536
or makes it impossible to then talk about that

1178
00:52:29.536 --> 00:52:31.494
in the third book, let's say.

1179
00:52:31.494 --> 00:52:34.030
Well, as somebody who's read all your books

1180
00:52:34.030 --> 00:52:36.483
and is a big fan of your writing,

1181
00:52:37.500 --> 00:52:42.500
in reading this book, what struck me is how confident it is.

1182
00:52:42.767 --> 00:52:46.210
I think it's your most confident book.

1183
00:52:46.210 --> 00:52:50.560
It's very strident in its directness.

1184
00:52:50.560 --> 00:52:53.110
It's like a call to action, the whole thing.

1185
00:52:53.110 --> 00:52:57.600
And I think like in thinking about your previous books,

1186
00:52:57.600 --> 00:53:00.360
they feel a little bit more kind of observational

1187
00:53:00.360 --> 00:53:03.610
or cautionary, like, don't do this, be careful of this.

1188
00:53:03.610 --> 00:53:05.320
This one is very muscular.

1189
00:53:05.320 --> 00:53:07.500
It's like an active verb.

1190
00:53:07.500 --> 00:53:09.540
It's very aggressive.

1191
00:53:09.540 --> 00:53:12.540
And maybe that's an act of courage in and of itself.

1192
00:53:12.540 --> 00:53:16.770
But my sense was he's really like found his voice

1193
00:53:16.770 --> 00:53:18.110
in this vein.

1194
00:53:18.110 --> 00:53:20.310
And there was no,

1195
00:53:20.310 --> 00:53:22.600
not that there was hesitation in your earlier books,

1196
00:53:22.600 --> 00:53:27.600
but it just felt like it was very confident

1197
00:53:28.390 --> 00:53:29.930
and kind of fully formed.

1198
00:53:29.930 --> 00:53:31.040
Does that make sense to you?

1199
00:53:31.040 --> 00:53:32.100
Do you have a sense of that?

1200
00:53:32.100 --> 00:53:32.933
It does.

1201
00:53:32.933 --> 00:53:33.766
Yeah, no, it makes sense.

1202
00:53:33.766 --> 00:53:34.599
I don't know-

1203
00:53:34.599 --> 00:53:35.590
Because it's kinda like do this

1204
00:53:35.590 --> 00:53:36.660
and here's what you're gonna do

1205
00:53:36.660 --> 00:53:38.510
and this is the way it's gonna be.

1206
00:53:38.510 --> 00:53:40.440
And like and then it would end with a question

1207
00:53:40.440 --> 00:53:42.067
like, are you gonna rise to the occasion?

1208
00:53:42.067 --> 00:53:44.220
And I was like, "Holy shit!" (laughs)

1209
00:53:44.220 --> 00:53:48.150
I feel, it's funny this a weird namedrop,

1210
00:53:48.150 --> 00:53:50.810
but Matthew McConaughey was nice enough to read the book

1211
00:53:50.810 --> 00:53:51.770
in galley form,

1212
00:53:51.770 --> 00:53:53.697
and he made me get rid of most of the questions.

1213
00:53:53.697 --> 00:53:55.300
Oh, he did?

1214
00:53:55.300 --> 00:53:56.470
He was like, he gave you this really good note.

1215
00:53:56.470 --> 00:53:57.790
He's like, "You're ending on a question

1216
00:53:57.790 --> 00:54:01.720
as if it is up for discussion."

1217
00:54:01.720 --> 00:54:02.870
And I said, "Oh, that's totally right."

1218
00:54:02.870 --> 00:54:03.703
Take off the table.

1219
00:54:03.703 --> 00:54:06.631
I love that you're getting notes from McConaughey.

1220
00:54:06.631 --> 00:54:07.464
That's-

1221
00:54:07.464 --> 00:54:08.297
It was a surreal.

1222
00:54:08.297 --> 00:54:09.257
I sent him the book

1223
00:54:09.257 --> 00:54:10.991
and I was hoping he would give me a blurb

1224
00:54:10.991 --> 00:54:12.370
because I had blurbed his book.

1225
00:54:12.370 --> 00:54:13.203
And I was like,

1226
00:54:13.203 --> 00:54:15.763
and like you can blurb a book without reading it.

1227
00:54:15.763 --> 00:54:18.233
Like so I was I was expecting that.

1228
00:54:19.250 --> 00:54:22.370
And he gave me like full like chapter-by-chapter notes.

1229
00:54:22.370 --> 00:54:24.133
It was incredible.

1230
00:54:24.133 --> 00:54:27.830
But I do feel like you get to,

1231
00:54:27.830 --> 00:54:29.230
it was a new feeling on the book,

1232
00:54:29.230 --> 00:54:34.230
where like I felt like I had all the powers that I needed.

1233
00:54:35.722 --> 00:54:39.360
Like it all come together.

1234
00:54:39.360 --> 00:54:43.150
Like there wasn't any doubt in my mind that I could do it

1235
00:54:43.150 --> 00:54:46.290
or that I thought I knew what I was talking about or not.

1236
00:54:46.290 --> 00:54:50.800
Like it just it was like

1237
00:54:53.290 --> 00:54:56.470
just the sort of process took over, I guess.

1238
00:54:56.470 --> 00:54:57.303
Do you know what I mean?

1239
00:54:57.303 --> 00:54:59.960
Like in the way that an athlete in like the fourth quarter

1240
00:54:59.960 --> 00:55:03.760
just sort of goes into it like a different lane

1241
00:55:03.760 --> 00:55:06.570
or gear or something, that just sort of happened.

1242
00:55:06.570 --> 00:55:09.830
And I think it was partly the uniqueness

1243
00:55:09.830 --> 00:55:12.380
of the circumstances of what was happening in the world.

1244
00:55:12.380 --> 00:55:14.840
And so, like that's something I wanna preserve.

1245
00:55:14.840 --> 00:55:17.142
But I also think it was just like

1246
00:55:17.142 --> 00:55:20.270
I've done this now a lot of times.

1247
00:55:20.270 --> 00:55:25.270
And so, all of the consciousness of it slipped away

1248
00:55:28.160 --> 00:55:29.573
and I could just do it.

1249
00:55:30.920 --> 00:55:33.710
Yeah, it felt like there was no,

1250
00:55:33.710 --> 00:55:36.800
like whatever you were holding back in earlier books,

1251
00:55:36.800 --> 00:55:38.740
the floodgates opened a little bit.

1252
00:55:38.740 --> 00:55:39.730
I know, I guess I-

1253
00:55:39.730 --> 00:55:43.850
Just because I think what's sort of unique

1254
00:55:43.850 --> 00:55:48.520
about your books is they're sort of genre-fluid.

1255
00:55:48.520 --> 00:55:51.250
Like they're they're philosophical treatises,

1256
00:55:51.250 --> 00:55:52.500
they're books about history,

1257
00:55:52.500 --> 00:55:54.310
but they're also self-help books.

1258
00:55:54.310 --> 00:55:59.250
And I feel like the kind of the self-help vector

1259
00:55:59.250 --> 00:56:01.450
kind of expanded a little bit in this one

1260
00:56:01.450 --> 00:56:06.450
and you are not shy about like being direct in your counsel.

1261
00:56:06.490 --> 00:56:09.333
The one thing that the unfair advantage of this book

1262
00:56:09.333 --> 00:56:13.190
that I will have to find a way to compensate for

1263
00:56:13.190 --> 00:56:16.850
in the other three books is that courage

1264
00:56:16.850 --> 00:56:21.350
is like the most primal thing

1265
00:56:21.350 --> 00:56:25.410
that like of all the virtues, of all the themes of history,

1266
00:56:25.410 --> 00:56:27.733
of humanity, literature,

1267
00:56:28.860 --> 00:56:31.180
courage is the most consistent

1268
00:56:31.180 --> 00:56:33.313
and universal of all the things.

1269
00:56:35.040 --> 00:56:39.970
And you're able to draw from the greatest stories

1270
00:56:39.970 --> 00:56:41.123
of all time.

1271
00:56:42.840 --> 00:56:45.100
And conversely, when you're talking about cowardice,

1272
00:56:45.100 --> 00:56:47.800
you're usually drawing on some of the most infamous

1273
00:56:47.800 --> 00:56:50.890
and shameful moments of all time.

1274
00:56:50.890 --> 00:56:54.637
And so, there is something magical about that.

1275
00:56:58.380 --> 00:56:59.560
Well, there's also,

1276
00:56:59.560 --> 00:57:03.910
there's a kind of masculinity about it

1277
00:57:03.910 --> 00:57:06.296
that I think informed the pros.

1278
00:57:06.296 --> 00:57:08.190
I guess, although that was something

1279
00:57:08.190 --> 00:57:09.310
I really thought about

1280
00:57:09.310 --> 00:57:11.880
because we tend to think of courage

1281
00:57:11.880 --> 00:57:13.620
as physical courage, right?

1282
00:57:13.620 --> 00:57:16.780
Like courage, as you said, courage is often seen

1283
00:57:16.780 --> 00:57:18.180
as a masculine virtue,

1284
00:57:18.180 --> 00:57:20.480
although actually in Latin,

1285
00:57:20.480 --> 00:57:24.883
it's sort of a a non-gendered word, like it just is.

1286
00:57:25.966 --> 00:57:28.845
But that virtue just is.

1287
00:57:28.845 --> 00:57:31.210
They're not different for men or women.

1288
00:57:31.210 --> 00:57:32.790
And in fact, one of the cool things about Stoicism

1289
00:57:32.790 --> 00:57:35.610
is the sort of great stoic teacher

1290
00:57:35.610 --> 00:57:38.450
Gaius Musonius Rufus who teaches Marcus Aurelius

1291
00:57:38.450 --> 00:57:39.937
and sort of teaches Epictetus

1292
00:57:39.937 --> 00:57:42.590
and a bunch of the other great Stoics.

1293
00:57:42.590 --> 00:57:44.340
He's like one of the first philosophers

1294
00:57:44.340 --> 00:57:46.540
to advocate for like sort of equal training

1295
00:57:46.540 --> 00:57:47.373
for men and women,

1296
00:57:47.373 --> 00:57:49.510
basically saying like it doesn't matter.

1297
00:57:49.510 --> 00:57:51.810
It's like virtue is virtue.

1298
00:57:51.810 --> 00:57:53.970
What we do in our lives might be different.

1299
00:57:53.970 --> 00:57:55.420
But like he's like, you don't care

1300
00:57:55.420 --> 00:57:57.350
what the gender of a horse is.

1301
00:57:57.350 --> 00:57:59.510
Like does it run fast?

1302
00:57:59.510 --> 00:58:01.030
That's what matters.

1303
00:58:01.030 --> 00:58:02.130
Does the dog hunt?

1304
00:58:02.130 --> 00:58:06.133
It doesn't matter if it's a male or a female dog.

1305
00:58:07.290 --> 00:58:09.450
So, there is a sort of universality in it.

1306
00:58:09.450 --> 00:58:13.330
But I did really wanna make it clear.

1307
00:58:13.330 --> 00:58:16.300
And how I pick the stories was a big part of that

1308
00:58:16.300 --> 00:58:21.090
was like this isn't just running into battle

1309
00:58:21.090 --> 00:58:21.923
or a burning building,

1310
00:58:21.923 --> 00:58:23.530
though, of course, men and women both do that.

1311
00:58:23.530 --> 00:58:27.280
But it's not just physical strength.

1312
00:58:27.280 --> 00:58:29.520
That's not what courage is.

1313
00:58:29.520 --> 00:58:31.670
And that's why I deliberately open the book

1314
00:58:31.670 --> 00:58:34.070
with Florence Nightingale as the main character.

1315
00:58:34.070 --> 00:58:37.790
So, it's not war that I'm celebrating,

1316
00:58:37.790 --> 00:58:42.480
but like the person who is courageous enough

1317
00:58:42.480 --> 00:58:47.480
and caring enough to focus on like that damage that war does

1318
00:58:48.190 --> 00:58:51.510
and helping the victims of that tragedy

1319
00:58:52.800 --> 00:58:54.688
just as much as like the people rushing

1320
00:58:54.688 --> 00:58:56.320
into that cavalry charge.

1321
00:58:56.320 --> 00:58:58.930
Yeah, we should probably like define our terms

1322
00:58:58.930 --> 00:59:00.270
and explain what courage is.

1323
00:59:00.270 --> 00:59:04.500
But the book basically opens with disabusing people

1324
00:59:04.500 --> 00:59:06.736
of this idea that there are two different kinds of courage,

1325
00:59:06.736 --> 00:59:10.890
physical and emotional, and yet in truth, it's one thing.

1326
00:59:10.890 --> 00:59:12.940
It's when you put your ass on the line.

1327
00:59:14.030 --> 00:59:17.080
So, walk me through like

1328
00:59:18.150 --> 00:59:20.560
what the four cardinal virtues are,

1329
00:59:20.560 --> 00:59:22.340
why they're called cardinal virtues,

1330
00:59:22.340 --> 00:59:25.580
and why you decided to write this series of books

1331
00:59:25.580 --> 00:59:26.730
on this subject matter.

1332
00:59:26.730 --> 00:59:29.100
A sort of a well-known known fact, actually,

1333
00:59:29.100 --> 00:59:30.710
so I open the book with this story

1334
00:59:30.710 --> 00:59:33.300
of the choice of Hercules,

1335
00:59:33.300 --> 00:59:35.770
which is Hercules comes to the crossroads.

1336
00:59:35.770 --> 00:59:38.730
And he's basically given the opportunity for the easy way

1337
00:59:38.730 --> 00:59:40.960
or the hard way, vice and virtue.

1338
00:59:40.960 --> 00:59:42.090
What does he choose?

1339
00:59:42.090 --> 00:59:42.923
And he chooses the hard way.

1340
00:59:42.923 --> 00:59:44.373
And this is why he becomes great.

1341
00:59:45.360 --> 00:59:49.603
But that story is actually, Zeno is the founder of Stoicism,

1342
00:59:50.450 --> 00:59:52.280
gets in this shipwreck and he loses everything

1343
00:59:52.280 --> 00:59:54.210
and he washes up in Athens.

1344
00:59:54.210 --> 00:59:56.370
And he walks into a bookstore

1345
00:59:56.370 --> 00:59:59.650
as the bookseller is reading that story.

1346
00:59:59.650 --> 01:00:03.360
So, the inception, the beginning of Stoicism

1347
01:00:03.360 --> 01:00:06.630
is actually that story, which I opened this book with.

1348
01:00:06.630 --> 01:00:09.320
It's a story that I think it goes back to Socrates.

1349
01:00:09.320 --> 01:00:11.230
But just the idea that we have a choice,

1350
01:00:11.230 --> 01:00:12.573
easy way or the hard way.

1351
01:00:13.480 --> 01:00:15.010
What you can get away with

1352
01:00:15.010 --> 01:00:18.590
versus what you demand of yourself.

1353
01:00:18.590 --> 01:00:20.865
And the four cardinal virtues

1354
01:00:20.865 --> 01:00:24.760
is that when people hear the word virtue,

1355
01:00:24.760 --> 01:00:27.170
they don't really know what that means.

1356
01:00:27.170 --> 01:00:29.670
And they often think it's like a religious thing

1357
01:00:29.670 --> 01:00:32.940
or they think it's like not having fun or something.

1358
01:00:32.940 --> 01:00:35.260
For the ancients, particularly the Stoics,

1359
01:00:35.260 --> 01:00:37.730
there were really four virtues:

1360
01:00:37.730 --> 01:00:42.730
courage, temperance or self-discipline, justice, and wisdom.

1361
01:00:43.110 --> 01:00:45.460
And the idea being that any and all situations

1362
01:00:45.460 --> 01:00:48.710
call for one or all of those virtues,

1363
01:00:48.710 --> 01:00:51.100
they all interconnect and kind of check

1364
01:00:51.100 --> 01:00:52.653
and enhance each other.

1365
01:00:54.970 --> 01:00:58.090
But when you hear that word, cardinal virtue,

1366
01:00:58.090 --> 01:01:00.700
which is sort of their universal term,

1367
01:01:00.700 --> 01:01:01.533
they're the stoic virtues,

1368
01:01:01.533 --> 01:01:04.130
but they're referred to as a cardinal virtues.

1369
01:01:04.130 --> 01:01:06.840
Cardinal just comes from the Latin word, cardo,

1370
01:01:06.840 --> 01:01:08.110
which means hinge.

1371
01:01:08.110 --> 01:01:10.460
So, the idea is like everything hinges

1372
01:01:10.460 --> 01:01:11.730
on those four virtues.

1373
01:01:11.730 --> 01:01:15.004
It's not a like a cardinal from the Catholic Church-

1374
01:01:15.004 --> 01:01:16.950
Yeah, I always assumed that it came from the Vatican.

1375
01:01:16.950 --> 01:01:18.103
Yes, me too.

1376
01:01:18.103 --> 01:01:20.780
And then to think, oh, no, it predates this

1377
01:01:20.780 --> 01:01:22.530
by like hundreds of years.

1378
01:01:22.530 --> 01:01:23.363
Right.

1379
01:01:24.310 --> 01:01:25.520
And the idea behind,

1380
01:01:25.520 --> 01:01:30.140
like what got you fascinated in exploring this terrain

1381
01:01:30.140 --> 01:01:31.263
in book format?

1382
01:01:32.210 --> 01:01:34.580
I think what I love about the cardinal virtues

1383
01:01:34.580 --> 01:01:36.510
is that it's as clear as we,

1384
01:01:36.510 --> 01:01:39.480
it's as close as we get in philosophy

1385
01:01:39.480 --> 01:01:42.003
to like the Ten Commandments, right?

1386
01:01:42.003 --> 01:01:46.780
Like so much of philosophy is just sort of vague or general.

1387
01:01:46.780 --> 01:01:50.290
It doesn't say like, do this, don't do that.

1388
01:01:50.290 --> 01:01:52.360
And what I really like about the four virtues

1389
01:01:52.360 --> 01:01:56.570
is it's inherently like do this, don't do this.

1390
01:01:56.570 --> 01:01:59.750
Like act with courage, which means don't be a coward, right?

1391
01:01:59.750 --> 01:02:01.220
Like do the right thing,

1392
01:02:01.220 --> 01:02:04.283
which means don't do the unjust, wrong thing.

1393
01:02:05.214 --> 01:02:08.490
Moderation, temperance just means like nothing in excess.

1394
01:02:08.490 --> 01:02:10.003
Although sometimes not at all.

1395
01:02:11.850 --> 01:02:15.990
And so, to me, what I really wanted to do in this series

1396
01:02:15.990 --> 01:02:20.090
is sort of explore what each of those virtues mean

1397
01:02:20.090 --> 01:02:24.383
and sort of in a demonstrable, like memorable way.

1398
01:02:25.220 --> 01:02:27.280
So, going into this book,

1399
01:02:27.280 --> 01:02:30.370
courage being kind of the preeminent of the virtues

1400
01:02:30.370 --> 01:02:33.045
makes sense to start here.

1401
01:02:33.045 --> 01:02:33.878
You can't have them.

1402
01:02:33.878 --> 01:02:35.510
You can't have any of the virtues without courage.

1403
01:02:35.510 --> 01:02:37.590
Without courage, of course.

1404
01:02:37.590 --> 01:02:40.520
We all on some level, know what courage is.

1405
01:02:40.520 --> 01:02:44.140
We practice it, we avoid it, we do all the things.

1406
01:02:44.140 --> 01:02:48.570
What did you learn that surprised you about courage

1407
01:02:48.570 --> 01:02:51.370
in this kind of deeper exploration?

1408
01:02:51.370 --> 01:02:52.600
Well, we think about courage,

1409
01:02:52.600 --> 01:02:56.181
it's like we all know what it is, we all admire it,

1410
01:02:56.181 --> 01:02:59.840
we all now what it can do,

1411
01:02:59.840 --> 01:03:01.720
and yet it's relatively rare.

1412
01:03:01.720 --> 01:03:02.980
Like it's one of those weird things,

1413
01:03:02.980 --> 01:03:04.420
where it's like we're all in agreement

1414
01:03:04.420 --> 01:03:06.020
that courage is important,

1415
01:03:06.020 --> 01:03:08.678
and then we're all sort of looking around being like,

1416
01:03:08.678 --> 01:03:11.247
"Why aren't people more courageous?"

1417
01:03:12.170 --> 01:03:16.703
We seem to ask ourselves that question less, right?

1418
01:03:16.703 --> 01:03:17.536
You know what I mean?

1419
01:03:17.536 --> 01:03:20.020
We all have strong opinions about the lack of courage

1420
01:03:20.020 --> 01:03:24.400
of our elected officials or public figures or whatever.

1421
01:03:24.400 --> 01:03:27.790
But we're very rarely holding ourselves

1422
01:03:27.790 --> 01:03:31.023
to the standard that we're asking them.

1423
01:03:31.960 --> 01:03:36.100
I think what happens is that we look at it

1424
01:03:36.100 --> 01:03:39.140
and say, well, there's a misalignment of incentives.

1425
01:03:39.140 --> 01:03:41.520
If the incentives were properly established,

1426
01:03:41.520 --> 01:03:43.100
then people would be more courageous.

1427
01:03:43.100 --> 01:03:47.750
But obviously, courage means acting

1428
01:03:47.750 --> 01:03:50.080
in the face of incentives

1429
01:03:50.080 --> 01:03:52.210
that perhaps are not aligned in your favor.

1430
01:03:52.210 --> 01:03:54.064
That is what courage is.

1431
01:03:54.064 --> 01:03:54.897
Totally.

1432
01:03:54.897 --> 01:03:55.730
Like we'll go like,

1433
01:03:55.730 --> 01:03:59.037
"Why won't this politician say what they really think?"

1434
01:03:59.037 --> 01:04:01.258
And we go, "Oh, they're just worried

1435
01:04:01.258 --> 01:04:03.250
about pissing off their base."

1436
01:04:03.250 --> 01:04:05.210
And then it goes to what we were just talking about,

1437
01:04:05.210 --> 01:04:08.280
which is you go, "Well, I don't wanna say this

1438
01:04:08.280 --> 01:04:11.080
because it will upset my audience."

1439
01:04:11.080 --> 01:04:14.500
Like, huh, there's a fun double standard, right?

1440
01:04:14.500 --> 01:04:16.330
Like we regularly expect other people,

1441
01:04:16.330 --> 01:04:19.200
like why isn't LeBron James speaking out about this?

1442
01:04:19.200 --> 01:04:21.250
And we go, "It's because he's afraid

1443
01:04:21.250 --> 01:04:22.760
of losing his endorsements."

1444
01:04:22.760 --> 01:04:27.180
And it's like and you won't tell your boss the truth

1445
01:04:27.180 --> 01:04:29.500
in the weekly conference call

1446
01:04:30.340 --> 01:04:32.040
because you don't wanna get on his bad side.

1447
01:04:32.040 --> 01:04:33.370
It's just easier not to.

1448
01:04:33.370 --> 01:04:34.203
Exactly.

1449
01:04:34.203 --> 01:04:35.650
So, we're all doing it on some level.

1450
01:04:35.650 --> 01:04:39.770
I think the biggest kind of take-away or epiphany

1451
01:04:39.770 --> 01:04:41.350
in the most general sense from the book

1452
01:04:41.350 --> 01:04:46.200
is this idea that courage isn't some trait

1453
01:04:46.200 --> 01:04:48.051
that we're born with or not,

1454
01:04:48.051 --> 01:04:50.890
that it's, in fact, a practice.

1455
01:04:50.890 --> 01:04:52.860
And it's something that we can cultivate

1456
01:04:52.860 --> 01:04:54.460
through the doing of it.

1457
01:04:54.460 --> 01:04:57.980
And the commitment to that practice

1458
01:04:57.980 --> 01:05:02.030
is what, of course, breeds a deeper capacity for the doing.

1459
01:05:02.030 --> 01:05:05.310
Yeah, Aristotle talks about how you acquire the virtues

1460
01:05:05.310 --> 01:05:06.970
by doing them, right?

1461
01:05:06.970 --> 01:05:09.390
Like you become a builder by building stuff.

1462
01:05:09.390 --> 01:05:12.720
You become courageous by regularly acting with courage

1463
01:05:12.720 --> 01:05:14.524
in things big and small.

1464
01:05:14.524 --> 01:05:19.490
And you don't get it by criticizing other people's courage

1465
01:05:19.490 --> 01:05:22.350
and you don't get it by waiting for some magical moment

1466
01:05:22.350 --> 01:05:26.510
where like because it really counts now, you'll do it.

1467
01:05:26.510 --> 01:05:28.430
Sort of, as you said, it's like in sports,

1468
01:05:28.430 --> 01:05:31.520
like are you consistently doing it in practice,

1469
01:05:31.520 --> 01:05:33.520
then you'll probably do it in competition.

1470
01:05:33.520 --> 01:05:36.550
Are you consistently acting with courage in your life?

1471
01:05:36.550 --> 01:05:38.490
Then if you do find yourself

1472
01:05:38.490 --> 01:05:41.070
in some pivotal world-changing moment,

1473
01:05:41.070 --> 01:05:44.040
maybe you'll measure up to the task.

1474
01:05:44.040 --> 01:05:47.700
But the idea that you're suddenly going to do it

1475
01:05:47.700 --> 01:05:50.740
after a lifetime of like the easy road

1476
01:05:50.740 --> 01:05:54.870
is probably fooling yourself.

1477
01:05:54.870 --> 01:05:55.703
Yeah.

1478
01:05:55.703 --> 01:05:58.300
And we can't talk about courage or the practice of it

1479
01:05:58.300 --> 01:06:01.480
without fully understanding the impediment to it,

1480
01:06:01.480 --> 01:06:02.313
which is fear.

1481
01:06:02.313 --> 01:06:05.470
So, the book is broken down into these three sections:

1482
01:06:05.470 --> 01:06:08.900
fear, then courage, and then heroism.

1483
01:06:08.900 --> 01:06:12.950
You open with fear because fear is,

1484
01:06:12.950 --> 01:06:16.010
like reckoning with fear, understanding it, appreciating it,

1485
01:06:16.010 --> 01:06:18.480
being honest about yourself

1486
01:06:21.120 --> 01:06:24.640
to the extent that fear influences your decisions

1487
01:06:24.640 --> 01:06:29.640
is a predicate upon which courage can be founded.

1488
01:06:30.420 --> 01:06:32.502
I didn't say that correctly, but you understand what I mean?

1489
01:06:32.502 --> 01:06:33.335
Like-

1490
01:06:33.335 --> 01:06:34.168
There is no courage without fear.

1491
01:06:34.168 --> 01:06:35.001
Like let's say,

1492
01:06:35.001 --> 01:06:36.540
and I think there are some people,

1493
01:06:36.540 --> 01:06:37.820
it's like a disorder or whatever.

1494
01:06:37.820 --> 01:06:40.150
Let's say something gets scrambled in your brain

1495
01:06:40.150 --> 01:06:43.860
and you are no longer capable of feeling fear.

1496
01:06:43.860 --> 01:06:46.510
As you go through the world, are you being courageous?

1497
01:06:46.510 --> 01:06:47.604
Like no.

1498
01:06:47.604 --> 01:06:52.604
You're probably being reckless, but you're not,

1499
01:06:52.940 --> 01:06:53.773
the whole point

1500
01:06:53.773 --> 01:06:56.900
is that you're having to push through the doubts.

1501
01:06:56.900 --> 01:07:01.850
That you're aware of the danger and doing it anyway, right?

1502
01:07:01.850 --> 01:07:06.850
And so, fear is both the enemy, but also the opportunity.

1503
01:07:07.270 --> 01:07:08.870
Like if you don't feel fear,

1504
01:07:08.870 --> 01:07:10.807
if it's for certain,

1505
01:07:10.807 --> 01:07:15.160
like if you're starting the business

1506
01:07:15.160 --> 01:07:17.950
and you know it's guaranteed that you will be successful,

1507
01:07:17.950 --> 01:07:19.660
like if you can flash forward in the future

1508
01:07:19.660 --> 01:07:22.950
and know 100% it's gonna work out,

1509
01:07:22.950 --> 01:07:25.637
there's nothing to regret, it's-

1510
01:07:25.637 --> 01:07:26.868
There's no courage involved.

1511
01:07:26.868 --> 01:07:27.701
There's no courage.

1512
01:07:27.701 --> 01:07:28.761
There's no courage.

1513
01:07:28.761 --> 01:07:32.492
Like it's the fact that it could go either way

1514
01:07:32.492 --> 01:07:37.492
that makes it impressive, but also makes it meaningful.

1515
01:07:37.520 --> 01:07:39.530
So, help me make sense of the difference

1516
01:07:39.530 --> 01:07:43.510
between behavior that is provoked by fear,

1517
01:07:43.510 --> 01:07:44.690
because we're seeing a lot of that right now.

1518
01:07:44.690 --> 01:07:47.370
A lot of people behaving badly

1519
01:07:47.370 --> 01:07:50.370
because they're being motivated

1520
01:07:50.370 --> 01:07:53.460
by some kind of sure base fear

1521
01:07:53.460 --> 01:07:58.460
versus the more noble action of courage in the face of fear.

1522
01:07:59.110 --> 01:08:02.380
There's a great Faulkner quote where he says like,

1523
01:08:02.380 --> 01:08:05.850
be scared, you can't help that, but don't be afraid.

1524
01:08:05.850 --> 01:08:06.850
And I like that idea

1525
01:08:06.850 --> 01:08:08.490
because it ties into what the Stoics talk about,

1526
01:08:08.490 --> 01:08:10.650
which is like you're gonna be scared.

1527
01:08:10.650 --> 01:08:13.020
This is like a biological thing, right?

1528
01:08:13.020 --> 01:08:16.020
Like somebody jumps out from the corner.

1529
01:08:16.020 --> 01:08:17.793
They scare you or you fail.

1530
01:08:18.742 --> 01:08:21.400
You're gonna be scared by something.

1531
01:08:21.400 --> 01:08:25.623
There's uncertainty and doubt and newness and emotion.

1532
01:08:26.585 --> 01:08:28.620
But the question is like, what do you do after?

1533
01:08:28.620 --> 01:08:32.790
So, I think the problem is we have a lot of people

1534
01:08:32.790 --> 01:08:35.600
who are acting out of fear.

1535
01:08:35.600 --> 01:08:38.210
They wanna either admit that they're scared

1536
01:08:38.210 --> 01:08:41.750
or they've just given themselves over to that fear.

1537
01:08:41.750 --> 01:08:43.590
They've become okay with it.

1538
01:08:43.590 --> 01:08:45.070
And I think that's the problem.

1539
01:08:45.070 --> 01:08:48.300
So, it's like you can be angry.

1540
01:08:48.300 --> 01:08:50.389
Just don't do things out of anger, right?

1541
01:08:50.389 --> 01:08:53.840
Like somebody hurts you or pisses you off

1542
01:08:53.840 --> 01:08:54.780
or screws you over,

1543
01:08:54.780 --> 01:08:55.720
it's totally normal

1544
01:08:55.720 --> 01:08:58.460
that you would have a negative opinion about that.

1545
01:08:58.460 --> 01:09:03.410
That Stoicism is not becoming this Buddha-like figure

1546
01:09:03.410 --> 01:09:05.890
where you feel nothing about this,

1547
01:09:05.890 --> 01:09:07.850
which isn't fair to put it, but you get what I'm saying.

1548
01:09:07.850 --> 01:09:10.300
You don't become a robot.

1549
01:09:10.300 --> 01:09:15.000
It's that you check yourself before you take actions

1550
01:09:15.000 --> 01:09:17.900
primarily driven by that emotion.

1551
01:09:17.900 --> 01:09:18.733
Right.

1552
01:09:18.733 --> 01:09:21.500
Here's where courage and temperance overlap

1553
01:09:21.500 --> 01:09:22.890
in the Venn diagram, right?

1554
01:09:22.890 --> 01:09:23.723
Totally.

1555
01:09:23.723 --> 01:09:27.770
Because just acting boldly but reactively

1556
01:09:28.880 --> 01:09:31.560
isn't necessarily courage and probably isn't.

1557
01:09:31.560 --> 01:09:33.300
Well, that is the tension

1558
01:09:33.300 --> 01:09:35.400
between what's bold and what's rash,

1559
01:09:35.400 --> 01:09:37.410
what's courageous, what's reckless.

1560
01:09:37.410 --> 01:09:38.444
Right.

1561
01:09:38.444 --> 01:09:40.830
Like being circumspect, understanding the risks,

1562
01:09:40.830 --> 01:09:42.900
like the Bezos example that you gave.

1563
01:09:42.900 --> 01:09:44.660
And you see this throughout the book,

1564
01:09:44.660 --> 01:09:47.520
in the many examples that you offer,

1565
01:09:47.520 --> 01:09:52.090
including examples of military campaigns, et cetera,

1566
01:09:52.090 --> 01:09:56.050
where the risks are heavily evaluated.

1567
01:09:56.050 --> 01:09:57.650
There's still fear.

1568
01:09:57.650 --> 01:10:01.310
And the decision, the bold decision to move forward

1569
01:10:01.310 --> 01:10:02.810
is with that awareness.

1570
01:10:02.810 --> 01:10:07.250
Like the example that you give about MacArthur and Korea,

1571
01:10:07.250 --> 01:10:09.860
I thought was pretty instructive on that level.

1572
01:10:09.860 --> 01:10:14.750
Yeah, he's well-aware the odds are not in his favor,

1573
01:10:14.750 --> 01:10:19.010
but he's also aware that the odds are not impossible.

1574
01:10:19.010 --> 01:10:19.843
Right.

1575
01:10:19.843 --> 01:10:21.320
So, just explain that scenario.

1576
01:10:21.320 --> 01:10:25.890
So, when North Korea overruns South Korea,

1577
01:10:25.890 --> 01:10:28.221
basically the U.S. is sort of caught off guard.

1578
01:10:28.221 --> 01:10:33.221
And MacArthur proposes not just sort of this-

1579
01:10:34.003 --> 01:10:37.367
He's in charge of that Pacific theater at the time.

1580
01:10:37.367 --> 01:10:38.757
And he proposes not just like,

1581
01:10:38.757 --> 01:10:40.000
"Hey, we're gonna battle them back."

1582
01:10:40.000 --> 01:10:43.140
He proposes a sort of bold, visionary,

1583
01:10:43.140 --> 01:10:44.540
like sort of encircle.

1584
01:10:44.540 --> 01:10:48.010
And he basically wants to land troops at Inchon,

1585
01:10:48.010 --> 01:10:51.250
which is like sort of behind the enemy.

1586
01:10:51.250 --> 01:10:56.120
And it's an invasion, an amphibious landing

1587
01:10:56.120 --> 01:10:58.470
that almost everyone is opposed to

1588
01:10:58.470 --> 01:11:00.860
because success is not certain.

1589
01:11:00.860 --> 01:11:01.693
And it feels like-

1590
01:11:01.693 --> 01:11:02.870
And the port is so dangerous.

1591
01:11:02.870 --> 01:11:03.940
You can't bring a ship.

1592
01:11:03.940 --> 01:11:05.660
It's an incredibly narrow window.

1593
01:11:05.660 --> 01:11:09.610
It's like at this time on this date, it will work.

1594
01:11:09.610 --> 01:11:13.220
But if you miss it by 30 minutes, it's a bloodbath.

1595
01:11:13.220 --> 01:11:16.000
But he actually says, well, this is why it will work.

1596
01:11:16.000 --> 01:11:17.160
They won't.

1597
01:11:17.160 --> 01:11:19.193
It's like too crazy for them to expect.

1598
01:11:21.200 --> 01:11:24.610
And he says it basically hits like that line

1599
01:11:24.610 --> 01:11:25.530
in "Dumb and Dumber,"

1600
01:11:25.530 --> 01:11:28.647
where he's like, "You're telling me there's a chance."

1601
01:11:30.000 --> 01:11:33.240
And he basically sees that it's not impossible.

1602
01:11:33.240 --> 01:11:35.114
And therefore, it is possible.

1603
01:11:35.114 --> 01:11:37.390
And he believes in his ability to defy the odds,

1604
01:11:37.390 --> 01:11:39.200
which is, I think, an important part.

1605
01:11:39.200 --> 01:11:41.460
If you've done things

1606
01:11:41.460 --> 01:11:44.350
that other people have said are impossible to do,

1607
01:11:44.350 --> 01:11:47.660
it does give you confidence in your ability,

1608
01:11:47.660 --> 01:11:49.490
gives you a certain amount of courage.

1609
01:11:49.490 --> 01:11:52.000
Like to go to the idea of opening the book story,

1610
01:11:52.000 --> 01:11:54.040
there's I think, two parts of this relates to.

1611
01:11:54.040 --> 01:11:56.640
Number one, as far as risk, of course, it's risky.

1612
01:11:56.640 --> 01:11:58.060
It could fail.

1613
01:11:58.060 --> 01:12:00.910
But as soon as we decided to take the risk,

1614
01:12:00.910 --> 01:12:02.040
the next thing we did was like,

1615
01:12:02.040 --> 01:12:04.382
well, why do these things typically fail

1616
01:12:04.382 --> 01:12:09.100
and how do we derisk this situation as much as possible?

1617
01:12:09.100 --> 01:12:14.050
Opening, leasing half of it, starting small.

1618
01:12:14.050 --> 01:12:16.870
We made a bunch of decisions that took a risky thing

1619
01:12:16.870 --> 01:12:18.680
and made it less risky.

1620
01:12:18.680 --> 01:12:21.120
But then also, like I have some confidence

1621
01:12:21.120 --> 01:12:22.183
in my ability to do.

1622
01:12:22.183 --> 01:12:24.560
I have blind faith, but I have confidence in it

1623
01:12:24.560 --> 01:12:27.750
because I've done risky things before

1624
01:12:27.750 --> 01:12:32.750
that people said was impossible or was likely to fail.

1625
01:12:33.090 --> 01:12:35.420
And I have learned from that,

1626
01:12:35.420 --> 01:12:39.280
and I've also learned from my own capacity and capabilities.

1627
01:12:39.280 --> 01:12:43.230
So, it's like that is why making courage habit is important.

1628
01:12:43.230 --> 01:12:47.700
You're like, "Oh, I've done a cliff dive like this before."

1629
01:12:47.700 --> 01:12:50.790
It looks scarier than it actually is.

1630
01:12:50.790 --> 01:12:54.300
If you've never done it, then the moment comes

1631
01:12:54.300 --> 01:12:57.427
and you're totally unfamiliar with something like this.

1632
01:12:57.427 --> 01:13:01.400
Yeah, I think it's also important to understand

1633
01:13:01.400 --> 01:13:04.080
that not all courage is noble, right?

1634
01:13:04.080 --> 01:13:09.080
Like this idea that you could be technically courageous

1635
01:13:10.180 --> 01:13:12.040
in your action, but wrong-headed.

1636
01:13:12.040 --> 01:13:13.140
Like I'm thinking about

1637
01:13:13.140 --> 01:13:16.083
like the occupation of the capital, right?

1638
01:13:16.083 --> 01:13:18.730
Like all of those people, there's a saying

1639
01:13:18.730 --> 01:13:21.460
that like every man is right from his perspective.

1640
01:13:21.460 --> 01:13:23.130
So, those people would,

1641
01:13:23.130 --> 01:13:27.190
and the people that support them would say

1642
01:13:27.190 --> 01:13:30.000
that those are those are courageous individuals.

1643
01:13:30.000 --> 01:13:31.660
There's a real risk of death.

1644
01:13:31.660 --> 01:13:33.810
Real risk consequences.

1645
01:13:33.810 --> 01:13:35.982
They believe it was the right thing to do.

1646
01:13:35.982 --> 01:13:36.860
And they adjust in with the cause

1647
01:13:36.860 --> 01:13:38.350
from their point of view.

1648
01:13:38.350 --> 01:13:41.120
But there's an important caveat,

1649
01:13:41.120 --> 01:13:42.520
and this is the distinction in the book

1650
01:13:42.520 --> 01:13:46.130
between courage and the heroic.

1651
01:13:46.130 --> 01:13:47.903
Lord Byron has a great line.

1652
01:13:49.030 --> 01:13:52.520
He says that the cause makes all that hallows

1653
01:13:52.520 --> 01:13:55.850
or degrades courage in its fall.

1654
01:13:55.850 --> 01:13:59.760
So, if you're beating a police officer to death

1655
01:13:59.760 --> 01:14:01.900
with a blue lives matter flag,

1656
01:14:01.900 --> 01:14:04.686
like you're taking a real risk.

1657
01:14:04.686 --> 01:14:07.273
It's a real physical danger.

1658
01:14:08.440 --> 01:14:10.870
But it's not just a horrible cause.

1659
01:14:10.870 --> 01:14:14.810
It's a hypocritical, contradictory cause, right?

1660
01:14:14.810 --> 01:14:17.620
So, the decision, I tell the story

1661
01:14:17.620 --> 01:14:19.480
because I talked and researched it, obviously,

1662
01:14:19.480 --> 01:14:21.020
for my first book,

1663
01:14:21.020 --> 01:14:21.853
or sorry,

1664
01:14:21.853 --> 01:14:25.010
my first sort of narrative nonfiction book, "Conspiracy,"

1665
01:14:25.010 --> 01:14:29.090
I talk about the editors of Gawker resigning on principle

1666
01:14:30.140 --> 01:14:32.377
over this horrible story that they...

1667
01:14:32.377 --> 01:14:33.750
No, they resigned on principle

1668
01:14:33.750 --> 01:14:37.360
because the management of the publication

1669
01:14:37.360 --> 01:14:40.140
had unpublished a story, right?

1670
01:14:40.140 --> 01:14:42.920
And they felt like this was management crossing

1671
01:14:42.920 --> 01:14:45.420
an important like church and state line

1672
01:14:45.420 --> 01:14:48.120
between business and editorial.

1673
01:14:48.120 --> 01:14:50.883
Now, that line is important and it is real.

1674
01:14:52.060 --> 01:14:55.460
But management was unpublishing a story

1675
01:14:55.460 --> 01:14:59.300
that outed somebody as gay

1676
01:14:59.300 --> 01:15:02.440
who was being extorted by a gay porn star.

1677
01:15:02.440 --> 01:15:04.080
Like it was a horrible story

1678
01:15:04.080 --> 01:15:05.830
that should not have been published.

1679
01:15:05.830 --> 01:15:08.320
So, the fact that it was unpublished,

1680
01:15:08.320 --> 01:15:11.393
well, that is morally complicated.

1681
01:15:12.260 --> 01:15:17.260
The actual principal on the line here is a story

1682
01:15:18.190 --> 01:15:20.009
that shouldn't have been published.

1683
01:15:20.009 --> 01:15:22.700
And we see a lot of that, right?

1684
01:15:22.700 --> 01:15:24.830
Like one of the examples that really struck me

1685
01:15:24.830 --> 01:15:28.640
during the summer was,

1686
01:15:28.640 --> 01:15:31.810
you remember that horrible video of that man

1687
01:15:31.810 --> 01:15:36.280
at a Black Lives Matters protests in Buffalo.

1688
01:15:36.280 --> 01:15:38.117
He's like walking up to the police

1689
01:15:38.117 --> 01:15:40.540
and this police officer shoves him to the ground.

1690
01:15:40.540 --> 01:15:42.320
And you can hear that thickening,

1691
01:15:42.320 --> 01:15:46.130
that sickening thud of his head hitting the ground.

1692
01:15:46.130 --> 01:15:51.130
Well, that police officer was suspended for that naturally.

1693
01:15:51.450 --> 01:15:55.670
But then all of the police officers in his unit

1694
01:15:55.670 --> 01:15:58.003
resigned in solidarity.

1695
01:15:58.890 --> 01:16:03.890
So, yes, the idea of brotherhood or commitment

1696
01:16:04.120 --> 01:16:07.220
to members of your unit is important

1697
01:16:07.220 --> 01:16:11.030
and it takes courage to resign from a career or profession.

1698
01:16:11.030 --> 01:16:11.863
I don't think they quit their jobs.

1699
01:16:11.863 --> 01:16:14.490
They just resigned from this one unit.

1700
01:16:14.490 --> 01:16:16.483
But to do that takes courage.

1701
01:16:18.670 --> 01:16:23.230
But again, your courage is in service of protecting a person

1702
01:16:23.230 --> 01:16:26.883
who on video viciously assaulted an old man.

1703
01:16:29.000 --> 01:16:32.410
So, the cause is everything.

1704
01:16:32.410 --> 01:16:35.400
Courage in pursuit of crap, you go,

1705
01:16:35.400 --> 01:16:37.940
I mean, were there brave soldiers who fought for the South?

1706
01:16:37.940 --> 01:16:38.860
Of course.

1707
01:16:38.860 --> 01:16:41.650
Were there brave soldiers who fought for Germany or Japan?

1708
01:16:41.650 --> 01:16:42.600
Yes.

1709
01:16:42.600 --> 01:16:46.730
But we instinctively know there's something meaningless

1710
01:16:46.730 --> 01:16:50.540
about that courage because it was in furtherance

1711
01:16:50.540 --> 01:16:54.900
of like the worst causes of all time.

1712
01:16:54.900 --> 01:16:58.230
Right, so deployment of courage

1713
01:16:58.230 --> 01:17:00.540
in the pursuit of a wrong-headed goal

1714
01:17:00.540 --> 01:17:04.613
or a sort of ethically compromised aim

1715
01:17:07.740 --> 01:17:10.310
really confuses the matter,

1716
01:17:10.310 --> 01:17:12.920
because the person who's pursuing that aim

1717
01:17:12.920 --> 01:17:16.280
is not under any confusion morally.

1718
01:17:16.280 --> 01:17:21.020
Well, this is where justice and wisdom act upon courage.

1719
01:17:21.020 --> 01:17:21.853
Right?

1720
01:17:21.853 --> 01:17:26.220
So, if you have fallen prey to misinformation

1721
01:17:26.220 --> 01:17:29.700
and then feel like what you're pursuing is right,

1722
01:17:29.700 --> 01:17:32.970
well, it might feel right to you, but you're wrong.

1723
01:17:32.970 --> 01:17:36.490
And if what you're feeling,

1724
01:17:36.490 --> 01:17:40.970
what you are resisting the power of the state, let's say,

1725
01:17:40.970 --> 01:17:42.140
like or your profession

1726
01:17:42.140 --> 01:17:44.821
is trying to get you to do something

1727
01:17:44.821 --> 01:17:46.245
that you don't wanna do.

1728
01:17:46.245 --> 01:17:47.170
That's great risk to yourself.

1729
01:17:47.170 --> 01:17:48.686
Yeah, your livelihood.

1730
01:17:48.686 --> 01:17:51.250
Yeah, that takes courage.

1731
01:17:51.250 --> 01:17:53.910
But what you're protesting is your right

1732
01:17:53.910 --> 01:17:56.310
to infect other people with a deadly virus

1733
01:17:56.310 --> 01:17:59.940
or like insert other example, right?

1734
01:17:59.940 --> 01:18:01.320
Q1 on whatever.

1735
01:18:01.320 --> 01:18:05.720
Yeah, you're valiantly defending

1736
01:18:05.720 --> 01:18:07.870
not just something that's not true,

1737
01:18:07.870 --> 01:18:12.870
but something that is largely negative or destructive.

1738
01:18:13.330 --> 01:18:16.450
And so, these things all connect with each other

1739
01:18:16.450 --> 01:18:18.280
in a really important way.

1740
01:18:18.280 --> 01:18:22.920
Now, is there some room for someone being courageous

1741
01:18:22.920 --> 01:18:24.200
about something you disagree with?

1742
01:18:24.200 --> 01:18:25.420
Of course.

1743
01:18:25.420 --> 01:18:27.375
And it's hard to definitively say

1744
01:18:27.375 --> 01:18:29.410
what a good cause is about,

1745
01:18:29.410 --> 01:18:33.047
but there is Lincoln famously goes like,

1746
01:18:33.047 --> 01:18:36.120
"Look, the South thinks what they're fighting for is right."

1747
01:18:36.120 --> 01:18:38.760
But he's like, "If you think what is right

1748
01:18:38.760 --> 01:18:41.170
is stealing the labor and sweat

1749
01:18:41.170 --> 01:18:43.380
and blood and tears of other people,

1750
01:18:43.380 --> 01:18:44.730
I don't really know what to tell you."

1751
01:18:44.730 --> 01:18:45.563
Right. Yeah.

1752
01:18:45.563 --> 01:18:47.310
I think this gets at the crux

1753
01:18:47.310 --> 01:18:51.850
of our kind of current cultural moral dilemma,

1754
01:18:51.850 --> 01:18:54.080
because we're seeing the propagation

1755
01:18:54.080 --> 01:18:57.790
of so much misinformation that is weaponized

1756
01:18:57.790 --> 01:19:02.790
in many different ways and is now creating a situation

1757
01:19:04.430 --> 01:19:07.160
in which it's becoming increasingly difficult

1758
01:19:07.160 --> 01:19:10.420
to do proper sense-making and understand what's true

1759
01:19:10.420 --> 01:19:11.810
and what isn't,

1760
01:19:11.810 --> 01:19:15.130
and it's fomenting tribes of people

1761
01:19:15.130 --> 01:19:18.700
who are very right-minded in their goals and their aims

1762
01:19:18.700 --> 01:19:20.890
and are acting courageously.

1763
01:19:20.890 --> 01:19:23.967
And it's challenging to take a step back

1764
01:19:23.967 --> 01:19:28.280
and to try to objectively evaluate the landscape

1765
01:19:30.270 --> 01:19:33.520
from a kind of global umbrella perspective.

1766
01:19:33.520 --> 01:19:37.310
But ultimately, this is why courage is related to wisdom

1767
01:19:37.310 --> 01:19:39.670
in that it takes courage to pursue wisdom.

1768
01:19:39.670 --> 01:19:42.237
So, there's the great poem,

1769
01:19:42.237 --> 01:19:44.480
"The Charge of the Light Brigade,"

1770
01:19:44.480 --> 01:19:48.500
which is the poem about this sort of near suicidal charge

1771
01:19:48.500 --> 01:19:52.100
by this British cavalry regiment in the Crimean War.

1772
01:19:52.100 --> 01:19:56.470
And they're basically ordered to attack in a way

1773
01:19:56.470 --> 01:19:57.890
that was impossible to win,

1774
01:19:57.890 --> 01:19:59.923
over an objective that didn't matter.

1775
01:20:01.110 --> 01:20:02.300
And when you read about that,

1776
01:20:02.300 --> 01:20:05.710
it's this beautiful, beautiful, inspiring poem.

1777
01:20:05.710 --> 01:20:08.660
But when you really study "The Charge of the Light Brigade,"

1778
01:20:08.660 --> 01:20:09.960
it strikes you that,

1779
01:20:09.960 --> 01:20:12.920
so they they basically go on this suicidal charge

1780
01:20:12.920 --> 01:20:14.844
and they almost all die.

1781
01:20:14.844 --> 01:20:16.680
Few of them come back.

1782
01:20:16.680 --> 01:20:20.540
Like 600 leave, like 100 come back.

1783
01:20:20.540 --> 01:20:25.540
And like the men, instead of being angry,

1784
01:20:26.120 --> 01:20:28.750
instead of being like what was that,

1785
01:20:28.750 --> 01:20:32.560
they actually line up to like charge again.

1786
01:20:32.560 --> 01:20:37.560
Like it was easier for them to just follow orders

1787
01:20:38.330 --> 01:20:41.650
than to look in the mirror and question the orders, right?

1788
01:20:41.650 --> 01:20:43.610
So, like when we go like,

1789
01:20:43.610 --> 01:20:45.670
oh, yeah, this person on January 6th

1790
01:20:45.670 --> 01:20:48.120
or this person who's in this conspiracy theory

1791
01:20:48.120 --> 01:20:52.560
or taking this cause, they think that it's right.

1792
01:20:52.560 --> 01:20:55.370
They also have doubts.

1793
01:20:55.370 --> 01:20:59.300
Everyone around them is trying to give them the information.

1794
01:20:59.300 --> 01:21:03.250
And there's this force called cognitive dissonance.

1795
01:21:03.250 --> 01:21:06.100
The real fear, the real thing

1796
01:21:06.100 --> 01:21:08.460
that they're being cowardly about

1797
01:21:08.460 --> 01:21:13.360
is the admission of error or the admission of doubt, right?

1798
01:21:13.360 --> 01:21:16.170
So, like people get in a cult.

1799
01:21:16.170 --> 01:21:19.040
And then the court does something terrible.

1800
01:21:19.040 --> 01:21:23.373
And then none of the predictions of the court come true.

1801
01:21:24.230 --> 01:21:28.680
They can't go like, "Oh, man, I was fooled.

1802
01:21:28.680 --> 01:21:29.959
I was an idiot."

1803
01:21:29.959 --> 01:21:32.040
Yeah, the fear is you were wrong all along.

1804
01:21:32.040 --> 01:21:36.070
And the courage then would be to step outside of that

1805
01:21:36.070 --> 01:21:37.730
and recognize that.

1806
01:21:37.730 --> 01:21:39.630
Yeah, the scariest thing in the world

1807
01:21:39.630 --> 01:21:43.770
is admitting you were wrong or that you did something wrong.

1808
01:21:43.770 --> 01:21:47.150
And so, you can't the idea of like,

1809
01:21:47.150 --> 01:21:48.150
oh, they think they're right

1810
01:21:48.150 --> 01:21:49.800
and they're just pursuing it with courage.

1811
01:21:49.800 --> 01:21:51.230
Shouldn't we admire that?

1812
01:21:51.230 --> 01:21:52.850
Not only is it like,

1813
01:21:52.850 --> 01:21:55.020
no, it matters what are the consequences

