What did you acquire this money fortune platform for,

2074
01:33:11.290 --> 01:33:16.290
if not to apply it towards problems that need solutions?

2075
01:33:17.570 --> 01:33:19.620
And so, I think we just we need,

2076
01:33:19.620 --> 01:33:21.430
to me, that's a really important question.

2077
01:33:21.430 --> 01:33:23.300
And I think it's Hillel.

2078
01:33:23.300 --> 01:33:24.720
It's like if not me, then who?

2079
01:33:24.720 --> 01:33:26.320
If not now, than when?

2080
01:33:26.320 --> 01:33:28.992
Like if everyone just listened

2081
01:33:28.992 --> 01:33:31.410
to all the reasons not to do it

2082
01:33:31.410 --> 01:33:36.410
or looked at why the odds were impossible,

2083
01:33:36.420 --> 01:33:39.520
we would never have progress or change or breakthroughs.

2084
01:33:39.520 --> 01:33:40.993
Sure.

2085
01:33:40.993 --> 01:33:45.467
So, when you yolk courage to service

2086
01:33:47.026 --> 01:33:49.810
towards something greater than yourself,

2087
01:33:49.810 --> 01:33:52.827
that's really where heroism comes in, right?

2088
01:33:52.827 --> 01:33:53.660
Yeah.

2089
01:33:53.660 --> 01:33:55.050
This idea, I mean, I just got this book yesterday.

2090
01:33:55.050 --> 01:33:57.000
So, I did my best to read the whole thing.

2091
01:33:57.000 --> 01:33:58.290
I got to page 190,

2092
01:33:58.290 --> 01:34:02.700
which is like right where the heroism chapter starts.

2093
01:34:02.700 --> 01:34:07.170
But my intuition is that heroism is courage,

2094
01:34:07.170 --> 01:34:10.210
basically when it's about something greater

2095
01:34:10.210 --> 01:34:12.960
than one's individual kind of aspirations

2096
01:34:12.960 --> 01:34:14.263
or it's called serving.

2097
01:34:14.263 --> 01:34:15.497
It's courage plus.

2098
01:34:15.497 --> 01:34:17.530
It's like courage when you're not gonna be the recipient

2099
01:34:17.530 --> 01:34:19.000
of the benefits, right?

2100
01:34:19.000 --> 01:34:20.740
Like selflessness.

2101
01:34:20.740 --> 01:34:23.970
And again, it's not always like throwing yourself

2102
01:34:23.970 --> 01:34:25.210
on a grenade.

2103
01:34:25.210 --> 01:34:30.210
It can be like, hey, I'm gonna like reduce my margins

2104
01:34:33.350 --> 01:34:35.533
to pay my workers a fair wage.

2105
01:34:36.960 --> 01:34:40.300
I could easily get away with doing it in China

2106
01:34:40.300 --> 01:34:43.550
in a weaker sweatshop concentration camp.

2107
01:34:43.550 --> 01:34:46.730
But that's not the right thing to do,

2108
01:34:46.730 --> 01:34:48.973
so I'm gonna take the hit on that.

2109
01:34:50.570 --> 01:34:51.403
That's hard.

2110
01:34:51.403 --> 01:34:52.236
That's really hard.

2111
01:34:52.236 --> 01:34:54.580
And I think we saw during the pandemic.

2112
01:34:54.580 --> 01:34:56.990
But if I don't do this,

2113
01:34:56.990 --> 01:34:59.070
sure, I'll be a vector for the virus,

2114
01:34:59.070 --> 01:35:00.980
but that's bad for business.

2115
01:35:00.980 --> 01:35:03.200
Is there a tension between like staying in business

2116
01:35:03.200 --> 01:35:05.680
and the right thing?

2117
01:35:05.680 --> 01:35:06.513
Of course.

2118
01:35:06.513 --> 01:35:10.530
But can you on at least a regular basis,

2119
01:35:10.530 --> 01:35:15.045
choose people over profits?

2120
01:35:15.045 --> 01:35:18.310
Like I talk about Reed Hastings,

2121
01:35:18.310 --> 01:35:22.773
the courageous decision to jettison the DVD business to-

2122
01:35:24.290 --> 01:35:25.123
Netflix.

2123
01:35:25.123 --> 01:35:27.370
Yeah, to become a streaming company.

2124
01:35:27.370 --> 01:35:28.750
Immensely courageous.

2125
01:35:28.750 --> 01:35:30.560
Then you have the streaming company.

2126
01:35:30.560 --> 01:35:34.210
And Hasan Minhaj has an episode

2127
01:35:34.210 --> 01:35:38.320
about the killing of dissidents in Saudi Arabia.

2128
01:35:38.320 --> 01:35:40.750
And Saudi Arabia says take this down.

2129
01:35:40.750 --> 01:35:42.280
And you're like, okay.

2130
01:35:42.280 --> 01:35:46.320
Like what good is having a company worth a trillion dollars

2131
01:35:46.320 --> 01:35:50.610
if you can't stand up for people

2132
01:35:50.610 --> 01:35:52.860
who get sliced into tiny pieces?

2133
01:35:52.860 --> 01:35:55.940
Yeah, I had this conversation with with Bryan Fogel,

2134
01:35:55.940 --> 01:35:58.100
who directed "The Dissident,"

2135
01:35:58.100 --> 01:36:02.683
and his struggle to get his incredible documentary

2136
01:36:02.683 --> 01:36:05.050
platformed on any of these streaming services.

2137
01:36:05.050 --> 01:36:08.790
And none of them would touch it for that very reason.

2138
01:36:08.790 --> 01:36:13.790
Even Amazon, where Bezos has no love lost for the prince.

2139
01:36:14.588 --> 01:36:15.620
I mean, it was his employee.

2140
01:36:15.620 --> 01:36:16.453
Right.

2141
01:36:16.453 --> 01:36:17.390
Yeah.

2142
01:36:17.390 --> 01:36:21.050
So, still wouldn't take the movie

2143
01:36:21.050 --> 01:36:23.570
because they're protecting their base.

2144
01:36:23.570 --> 01:36:27.810
Because broadening their subscriptions worldwide

2145
01:36:27.810 --> 01:36:29.757
is more important than somebody's movie,

2146
01:36:29.757 --> 01:36:31.316
no matter how good it is.

2147
01:36:31.316 --> 01:36:32.860
Right.

2148
01:36:32.860 --> 01:36:34.070
But is it worth the risk.

2149
01:36:34.070 --> 01:36:35.477
I mean, Bezos would say,

2150
01:36:35.477 --> 01:36:37.603
"Well, that's just not a risk worth.

2151
01:36:38.460 --> 01:36:39.730
Like, why should I?

2152
01:36:39.730 --> 01:36:42.940
It's just one movie in my massive enterprise.'

2153
01:36:42.940 --> 01:36:43.773
Right. No. No.

2154
01:36:43.773 --> 01:36:47.060
And that's how we excuse moments of cowardice.

2155
01:36:47.060 --> 01:36:48.540
We always have our reasons.

2156
01:36:48.540 --> 01:36:50.220
And I don't mean to judge them specifically,

2157
01:36:50.220 --> 01:36:52.600
because to me, what you take from that is like,

2158
01:36:52.600 --> 01:36:55.770
well, where am I doing this in my own life?

2159
01:36:55.770 --> 01:36:57.220
That's where we should take from this.

2160
01:36:57.220 --> 01:36:59.290
But sometimes we use these examples

2161
01:36:59.290 --> 01:37:01.140
as a way of sort of producing clarity

2162
01:37:01.140 --> 01:37:02.450
that we can't see in our own lives.

2163
01:37:02.450 --> 01:37:07.250
But again, what the hell is the point

2164
01:37:07.250 --> 01:37:09.750
of being the richest person in the world

2165
01:37:09.750 --> 01:37:12.980
if you can't flex occasionally

2166
01:37:12.980 --> 01:37:16.303
over what's obviously the right thing?

2167
01:37:17.468 --> 01:37:20.417
Again, we always have reasons why it's not the right thing.

2168
01:37:20.417 --> 01:37:22.060
"But then I can't do this, this, or that,"

2169
01:37:22.060 --> 01:37:23.570
but it's usually rooted in

2170
01:37:24.410 --> 01:37:26.250
I don't wanna put up with the consequence.

2171
01:37:26.250 --> 01:37:27.510
Like I don't want the flak.

2172
01:37:27.510 --> 01:37:30.610
I mean, he would say it isn't even a function of courage.

2173
01:37:30.610 --> 01:37:32.220
It's just a risk analysis.

2174
01:37:32.220 --> 01:37:33.730
Yeah.

2175
01:37:33.730 --> 01:37:37.920
But at the core of it, what is being risked?

2176
01:37:37.920 --> 01:37:38.753
Right?

2177
01:37:38.753 --> 01:37:39.860
It's not you're going out of business.

2178
01:37:39.860 --> 01:37:43.030
It's like a slight decrease in profits

2179
01:37:43.030 --> 01:37:45.340
or it's a bunch of controversy

2180
01:37:45.340 --> 01:37:47.540
or it's a giant pain in the ass, right?

2181
01:37:47.540 --> 01:37:49.130
Like what we're actually,

2182
01:37:49.130 --> 01:37:52.000
this is why we have to really get into what we're afraid of.

2183
01:37:52.000 --> 01:37:55.110
Like, oh, it's a risk analysis, but what are you risking?

2184
01:37:55.110 --> 01:37:56.340
Like the risk is nothing.

2185
01:37:56.340 --> 01:37:58.150
And if you can't,

2186
01:37:58.150 --> 01:38:01.260
if not you, then who?

2187
01:38:01.260 --> 01:38:03.360
If you can't afford to risk it,

2188
01:38:03.360 --> 01:38:06.080
like how can anyone afford to risk anything ever?

2189
01:38:06.080 --> 01:38:09.340
Yeah, because you of all people need to lead the way

2190
01:38:09.340 --> 01:38:10.540
for the rest of us.

2191
01:38:10.540 --> 01:38:12.730
And so, throughout all of your books,

2192
01:38:12.730 --> 01:38:13.850
you're always very careful

2193
01:38:13.850 --> 01:38:18.850
to kind of avoid current examples,

2194
01:38:20.690 --> 01:38:21.670
because you want these things

2195
01:38:21.670 --> 01:38:23.320
to stand the test of time, et cetera.

2196
01:38:23.320 --> 01:38:26.100
But casting your glaze on like our current moment,

2197
01:38:26.100 --> 01:38:27.560
you mentioned Kaepernick then the men,

2198
01:38:27.560 --> 01:38:30.730
like who do you look at or see out in the world

2199
01:38:30.730 --> 01:38:33.070
and say that person is acting courageously

2200
01:38:33.070 --> 01:38:36.150
or that person is acting heroically?

2201
01:38:36.150 --> 01:38:38.030
Yeah, I mean, I think obviously,

2202
01:38:38.030 --> 01:38:41.220
we have a whole bunch of heroes who've done,

2203
01:38:41.220 --> 01:38:45.940
I mean, even you look at like the 12 or 13 service members

2204
01:38:45.940 --> 01:38:47.910
who just died in Afghanistan.

2205
01:38:47.910 --> 01:38:50.100
Like they knew that wasn't fun.

2206
01:38:50.100 --> 01:38:51.800
They signed up for it,

2207
01:38:51.800 --> 01:38:55.610
but they went into those crowds

2208
01:38:55.610 --> 01:38:59.130
to do one of the largest evacuations

2209
01:38:59.130 --> 01:39:02.700
and rescue operations in human history.

2210
01:39:02.700 --> 01:39:07.630
I mean, like more than 100,000 people were airlifted out

2211
01:39:07.630 --> 01:39:09.250
in a matter of days,

2212
01:39:09.250 --> 01:39:14.250
but at immense risk to the people on the ground, right?

2213
01:39:15.420 --> 01:39:19.370
And so, when I think we think about heroism specifically,

2214
01:39:19.370 --> 01:39:20.930
it's people who like literally

2215
01:39:20.930 --> 01:39:22.630
are putting their ass on the line.

2216
01:39:24.430 --> 01:39:29.040
To me, that's the stuff that we study those not,

2217
01:39:29.040 --> 01:39:32.710
to be like, oh, you have to enlist, but to be like again,

2218
01:39:32.710 --> 01:39:34.100
if they can,

2219
01:39:34.100 --> 01:39:39.100
if some 22-year-old woman like private in the Marines

2220
01:39:39.690 --> 01:39:44.690
can walk into a crowd filled with potential suicide bombers

2221
01:39:45.710 --> 01:39:47.900
to rescue women and children,

2222
01:39:47.900 --> 01:39:51.460
like you're telling me you can't speak up

2223
01:39:51.460 --> 01:39:52.760
about something you saw

2224
01:39:52.760 --> 01:39:57.720
or you can't you can't put aside a salary

2225
01:39:57.720 --> 01:39:59.590
to start your own business is too scary

2226
01:39:59.590 --> 01:40:01.860
or like you can't get up in front of a crowd and talk.

2227
01:40:01.860 --> 01:40:06.830
Or to see Afghan women in Afghanistan right now in Kabul

2228
01:40:06.830 --> 01:40:09.900
amidst the chaos of the Taliban occupation,

2229
01:40:09.900 --> 01:40:12.140
continuing to speak out.

2230
01:40:12.140 --> 01:40:14.350
I mean, the amount of risk,

2231
01:40:14.350 --> 01:40:16.970
like real risk that they're taking to do that

2232
01:40:16.970 --> 01:40:18.740
is just unbelievable.

2233
01:40:18.740 --> 01:40:19.573
Yeah.

2234
01:40:19.573 --> 01:40:24.220
Or yeah, any time you're putting your wellbeing

2235
01:40:25.400 --> 01:40:27.770
second to something bigger than yourself,

2236
01:40:27.770 --> 01:40:30.650
that's like what gets me going.

2237
01:40:30.650 --> 01:40:32.590
One of the minor examples I have in the book,

2238
01:40:32.590 --> 01:40:33.423
I sort of throw it,

2239
01:40:33.423 --> 01:40:35.480
but it's stuck with me since I heard about it,

2240
01:40:35.480 --> 01:40:39.026
is like the decision of like CVS to stop carrying cigarets

2241
01:40:39.026 --> 01:40:41.371
or like Chipotle can make more money

2242
01:40:41.371 --> 01:40:44.520
with crappy ingredients in their food.

2243
01:40:44.520 --> 01:40:47.730
Like when a business decides to be like, hey,

2244
01:40:47.730 --> 01:40:49.390
and look, is there a certain amount of marketing to it?

2245
01:40:49.390 --> 01:40:50.223
Sure.

2246
01:40:50.223 --> 01:40:53.360
But like, hey, like our commitment to quality

2247
01:40:53.360 --> 01:40:57.100
or ethics or our people is more important

2248
01:40:57.100 --> 01:41:01.820
than like bringing profits out of this part of the business.

2249
01:41:01.820 --> 01:41:04.580
I mean, Patagonia is a great example of that as well.

2250
01:41:04.580 --> 01:41:05.530
Totally.

2251
01:41:05.530 --> 01:41:06.363
I love that stuff.

2252
01:41:06.363 --> 01:41:07.608
Yeah.

2253
01:41:07.608 --> 01:41:10.520
And ultimately, it ends up being very much

2254
01:41:10.520 --> 01:41:11.880
in their self-interest.

2255
01:41:11.880 --> 01:41:13.180
It usually is, yes.

2256
01:41:13.180 --> 01:41:15.023
Like in a very outweighed manner.

2257
01:41:15.880 --> 01:41:18.850
That the irony is that like doing it

2258
01:41:18.850 --> 01:41:22.590
the shitty conventional way is also boring

2259
01:41:22.590 --> 01:41:26.900
and not particularly inspiring or cool.

2260
01:41:26.900 --> 01:41:30.930
And so, like the decision to do the like to make art

2261
01:41:30.930 --> 01:41:33.830
that everyone else is making is safer,

2262
01:41:33.830 --> 01:41:38.700
but it's also like probably the least likely

2263
01:41:38.700 --> 01:41:39.880
to be successful.

2264
01:41:39.880 --> 01:41:40.900
So, going out there.

2265
01:41:40.900 --> 01:41:44.130
Like getting out on a limb, like taking a risk

2266
01:41:44.130 --> 01:41:48.333
is usually in your self-interest.

2267
01:41:49.370 --> 01:41:52.640
So, if you have to drill down like the core concepts

2268
01:41:52.640 --> 01:41:54.920
that you want people to take away from this book,

2269
01:41:54.920 --> 01:41:56.150
what does that look like?

2270
01:41:56.150 --> 01:42:00.160
Well, I like the distinction between like being scared

2271
01:42:00.160 --> 01:42:01.180
and being afraid.

2272
01:42:01.180 --> 01:42:04.080
Being scared is the immediate reaction.

2273
01:42:04.080 --> 01:42:07.060
Being afraid is the rationalization

2274
01:42:07.060 --> 01:42:10.293
or the thing you refuse to do because of that fear.

2275
01:42:11.420 --> 01:42:14.517
There's a story I tell in the book about Theodore Roosevelt

2276
01:42:14.517 --> 01:42:16.910
and the decision to invite Booker T. Washington

2277
01:42:16.910 --> 01:42:18.970
to have dinner at the White House.

2278
01:42:18.970 --> 01:42:23.970
And he talks about how because he hesitated,

2279
01:42:24.150 --> 01:42:25.820
like because he thought for a second,

2280
01:42:25.820 --> 01:42:30.003
like what will this mean, is like why he needed to do it.

2281
01:42:30.930 --> 01:42:33.910
Steven Pressfield talks about how like resistance

2282
01:42:33.910 --> 01:42:37.513
is connected to like how important it is.

2283
01:42:38.350 --> 01:42:39.470
And so, if you're not feeling,

2284
01:42:39.470 --> 01:42:43.190
that's probably a sign you're like playing it really safe.

2285
01:42:43.190 --> 01:42:45.360
But what's really what's interesting about Roosevelt

2286
01:42:45.360 --> 01:42:49.730
is his will to greatness was not,

2287
01:42:49.730 --> 01:42:53.260
I mean, the Washington example is sort of standalone,

2288
01:42:53.260 --> 01:42:56.170
but in the context of his presidency,

2289
01:42:56.170 --> 01:43:00.410
he wasn't a president that faced dire circumstances

2290
01:43:00.410 --> 01:43:02.580
that compelled him to kind of overcome

2291
01:43:02.580 --> 01:43:03.700
or rise to the occasion.

2292
01:43:03.700 --> 01:43:06.320
Like he willed it out of whole cloth

2293
01:43:06.320 --> 01:43:09.770
during a period of relative serenity and calm,

2294
01:43:09.770 --> 01:43:10.890
which makes it, to me,

2295
01:43:10.890 --> 01:43:14.101
like even a more extraordinary capacity

2296
01:43:14.101 --> 01:43:16.480
for like what he's about.

2297
01:43:16.480 --> 01:43:18.407
Although the Doris Kearns Goodwin book,

2298
01:43:18.407 --> 01:43:20.440
"Leadership: In Turbulent Times" is a great book,

2299
01:43:20.440 --> 01:43:23.180
and she really focuses on the crises of his presidency

2300
01:43:23.180 --> 01:43:24.530
that I didn't quite know about.

2301
01:43:24.530 --> 01:43:27.030
I mean, he wins a Nobel Peace Prize

2302
01:43:27.030 --> 01:43:32.030
for negotiating peace between Russia and Japan,

2303
01:43:32.110 --> 01:43:33.760
which I didn't really know about.

2304
01:43:34.710 --> 01:43:38.330
He negotiates like a major strike between the coal mines

2305
01:43:38.330 --> 01:43:40.530
and the coal workers.

2306
01:43:40.530 --> 01:43:45.530
He faces down like massive corporate interests

2307
01:43:45.720 --> 01:43:47.273
in the trust-busting stuff.

2308
01:43:48.800 --> 01:43:51.880
So, it's interesting like, yeah, we often think

2309
01:43:51.880 --> 01:43:56.013
like courage is like, hey, we're invaded or-

2310
01:43:58.420 --> 01:44:03.390
Right, it's grown out of like some circumstance

2311
01:44:03.390 --> 01:44:04.773
beyond your control compels it.

2312
01:44:04.773 --> 01:44:06.000
I think it's also, though,

2313
01:44:06.000 --> 01:44:09.490
like the courage to like not kick the can down the road.

2314
01:44:09.490 --> 01:44:10.658
Like the courage to be like,

2315
01:44:10.658 --> 01:44:11.540
"Oh, I'm gonna deal with this problem.

2316
01:44:11.540 --> 01:44:12.770
It's not the sexiest problem,

2317
01:44:12.770 --> 01:44:15.630
it's not the most glamorous problem,

2318
01:44:15.630 --> 01:44:16.463
but like I'm gonna deal with it."

2319
01:44:16.463 --> 01:44:17.601
Like to me-

2320
01:44:17.601 --> 01:44:18.434
Yeah, that's another thing in the book, too,

2321
01:44:18.434 --> 01:44:19.267
like owning it,

2322
01:44:19.267 --> 01:44:20.710
like not shirking responsibility

2323
01:44:20.710 --> 01:44:23.360
and like understanding that the buck stops with you

2324
01:44:23.360 --> 01:44:25.670
and kind of being that guy.

2325
01:44:25.670 --> 01:44:27.910
To me, that's like the impressive thing with Biden

2326
01:44:27.910 --> 01:44:28.743
in Afghanistan.

2327
01:44:28.743 --> 01:44:30.980
And I don't know enough

2328
01:44:30.980 --> 01:44:33.600
to know whether he executed the withdrawal

2329
01:44:33.600 --> 01:44:34.830
as well as it could be done.

2330
01:44:34.830 --> 01:44:36.011
I don't think any of us do.

2331
01:44:36.011 --> 01:44:37.040
And I don't think we'll know yet.

2332
01:44:37.040 --> 01:44:39.400
It doesn't appear that it was executed very well.

2333
01:44:39.400 --> 01:44:41.970
It does not, but, again, who knows?

2334
01:44:41.970 --> 01:44:42.803
Right?

2335
01:44:42.803 --> 01:44:44.350
But I think you can put that to the side

2336
01:44:44.350 --> 01:44:49.350
and go like this dude gambled his presidency to end a thing

2337
01:44:49.490 --> 01:44:51.520
that should have been ended a long time ago.

2338
01:44:51.520 --> 01:44:54.090
Knowing it would be very unpopular

2339
01:44:54.090 --> 01:44:57.930
and not without its consequences.

2340
01:44:57.930 --> 01:44:59.150
That's right.

2341
01:44:59.150 --> 01:45:04.010
And the thing that each one of his predecessors bore

2342
01:45:04.010 --> 01:45:07.120
a far higher share of the blame

2343
01:45:07.120 --> 01:45:11.740
and the responsibility for the poor execution.

2344
01:45:11.740 --> 01:45:14.250
And so, to be like, I'm gonna own this.

2345
01:45:14.250 --> 01:45:16.550
Like I thought a lot about George Marshall

2346
01:45:16.550 --> 01:45:17.987
when I was writing the book,

2347
01:45:17.987 --> 01:45:19.350
and I used your dad's book as a source.

2348
01:45:19.350 --> 01:45:20.667
But his famous thing was like,

2349
01:45:20.667 --> 01:45:21.900
"We're not gonna fight the problem.

2350
01:45:21.900 --> 01:45:23.520
We're gonna decide it.

2351
01:45:23.520 --> 01:45:27.110
Like we're just gonna do something about this.

2352
01:45:27.110 --> 01:45:30.430
We're not gonna let it be somebody else's problem."

2353
01:45:30.430 --> 01:45:33.070
That's what leaders have to have the courage to do.

2354
01:45:33.070 --> 01:45:37.770
Because, again, if you're not, just don't be a leader.

2355
01:45:37.770 --> 01:45:38.603
Like I mean, if you're like,

2356
01:45:38.603 --> 01:45:41.010
"Hey, I'm not the kind of person

2357
01:45:41.010 --> 01:45:44.100
who likes to deal with unpopular or difficult problems,"

2358
01:45:44.100 --> 01:45:46.450
that's totally understandable.

2359
01:45:46.450 --> 01:45:50.228
You're not a good fit for being in charge, though.

2360
01:45:50.228 --> 01:45:51.061
(Rich laughs)

2361
01:45:51.061 --> 01:45:52.640
Like you know what I mean?

2362
01:45:52.640 --> 01:45:55.037
No judgment, but it's like if you're like,

2363
01:45:55.037 --> 01:45:58.040
"I don't like getting up in front of audiences,"

2364
01:45:58.040 --> 01:46:00.860
okay, don't be an actor or a public speaker.

2365
01:46:00.860 --> 01:46:02.713
Those are bad jobs for you.

2366
01:46:04.070 --> 01:46:05.980
You don't have to face everything,

2367
01:46:05.980 --> 01:46:10.020
but don't pick a job in which success

2368
01:46:10.020 --> 01:46:12.966
is entirely dependent on the things you don't like to do.

2369
01:46:12.966 --> 01:46:13.799
Right.

2370
01:46:13.799 --> 01:46:18.220
But also, courage is bred through repetition and practice.

2371
01:46:18.220 --> 01:46:21.450
So, putting yourselves in those uncomfortable situations

2372
01:46:21.450 --> 01:46:24.090
is the opportunity to grow,

2373
01:46:24.090 --> 01:46:25.880
which is what we're all here to do.

2374
01:46:25.880 --> 01:46:28.590
So, if there is a call to action out of this book,

2375
01:46:28.590 --> 01:46:32.620
it is to understand that you can cultivate this

2376
01:46:32.620 --> 01:46:35.810
by taking those tiny steps where the stakes are lower

2377
01:46:35.810 --> 01:46:39.736
to habituate yourself to this type of behavior.

2378
01:46:39.736 --> 01:46:41.550
It's like how do you think they got good at it?

2379
01:46:41.550 --> 01:46:42.550
It's by doing it.

2380
01:46:42.550 --> 01:46:43.383
By not-

2381
01:46:43.383 --> 01:46:45.930
Yeah, I mean, the example is always like Laird Hamilton

2382
01:46:45.930 --> 01:46:49.560
doesn't drop down on a 50-foot wave like on day one.

2383
01:46:49.560 --> 01:46:51.650
He walks up to it over decades.

2384
01:46:51.650 --> 01:46:53.270
Right. Right.

2385
01:46:53.270 --> 01:46:55.960
And it takes courage to tackle it in a small way.

2386
01:46:55.960 --> 01:47:00.583
But the nice part is that the momentum also builds courage.

2387
01:47:02.090 --> 01:47:04.150
I love how you and my dad

2388
01:47:05.360 --> 01:47:09.190
have this like relationship outside of me. (laughs)

2389
01:47:09.190 --> 01:47:12.580
And one of the things that you guys are kind of united on

2390
01:47:12.580 --> 01:47:16.270
or bond on is this whole thing about like statues.

2391
01:47:16.270 --> 01:47:17.340
Oh, yeah.

2392
01:47:17.340 --> 01:47:20.810
Like my dad is all about like getting this martial statue

2393
01:47:20.810 --> 01:47:21.650
in the Capitol.

2394
01:47:21.650 --> 01:47:24.520
It's like it's not going anywhere.

2395
01:47:24.520 --> 01:47:25.820
Oh, it's not happening?

2396
01:47:25.820 --> 01:47:26.760
I don't think it's gonna happen.

2397
01:47:26.760 --> 01:47:29.993
But to me, that's a big part of it, because,

2398
01:47:30.990 --> 01:47:33.270
and I did a piece we talked about responsibility earlier.

2399
01:47:33.270 --> 01:47:35.740
I was talking about I wrote a piece for "The Economist"

2400
01:47:35.740 --> 01:47:39.173
about how we need a statue of responsibility.

2401
01:47:40.300 --> 01:47:45.300
But like who we celebrate as our heroes is really important.

2402
01:47:47.030 --> 01:47:52.030
Like who we put up on display says a lot about who we are.

2403
01:47:52.840 --> 01:47:56.140
And it also, I think, has a big impact

2404
01:47:56.140 --> 01:47:58.430
on who we're going to be.

2405
01:47:58.430 --> 01:48:03.430
So, does our sort of lingering racism and racial issues,

2406
01:48:07.240 --> 01:48:10.590
is that partly rooted that a good chunk of the United States

2407
01:48:10.590 --> 01:48:14.933
has on its public property celebrations of like people

2408
01:48:16.130 --> 01:48:20.630
who were instrumental in defending

2409
01:48:20.630 --> 01:48:22.040
and propagating those ideas?

2410
01:48:22.040 --> 01:48:22.873
I do.

2411
01:48:22.873 --> 01:48:24.430
I think they're related.

2412
01:48:24.430 --> 01:48:28.090
Conversely, like is our inability

2413
01:48:28.090 --> 01:48:31.410
to celebrate unifying figures

2414
01:48:31.410 --> 01:48:34.460
whose courage is not in dispute

2415
01:48:34.460 --> 01:48:39.460
and who did fight more often than not for just causes.

2416
01:48:39.890 --> 01:48:43.523
Is that also holding us back, preventing us from.

2417
01:48:45.520 --> 01:48:47.850
Is it preventing ample inspiration?

2418
01:48:47.850 --> 01:48:49.810
Like I think about like who do I want my kids

2419
01:48:49.810 --> 01:48:51.827
to like walk down the street and see me like,

2420
01:48:51.827 --> 01:48:52.910
"Oh, that's so-and-so."

2421
01:48:52.910 --> 01:48:55.066
Like that's a great a Longfellow's

2422
01:48:55.066 --> 01:48:58.730
is the lives of all great men remind us

2423
01:48:58.730 --> 01:49:00.590
we can make our lives sublime.

2424
01:49:00.590 --> 01:49:04.230
I love the idea of like, who are we celebrating?

2425
01:49:04.230 --> 01:49:05.913
Like, who are our heroes?

2426
01:49:07.040 --> 01:49:10.120
And I think obviously sort of metal or bronze or marble

2427
01:49:10.120 --> 01:49:11.913
is like a way to do that.

2428
01:49:12.830 --> 01:49:15.440
Conversely, like who are villains?

2429
01:49:15.440 --> 01:49:17.510
They should not be not be celebrated.

2430
01:49:17.510 --> 01:49:19.690
Yeah, but the way that we define those

2431
01:49:19.690 --> 01:49:21.620
is very much in flux right now.

2432
01:49:21.620 --> 01:49:25.690
I mean, certainly, on the one hand, we have people

2433
01:49:25.690 --> 01:49:30.690
who are defending these statues of Confederate heroes,

2434
01:49:31.160 --> 01:49:32.130
et cetera.

2435
01:49:32.130 --> 01:49:34.970
On the other hand, we have people who are calling

2436
01:49:34.970 --> 01:49:38.620
for schools to change their name from Abraham Lincoln.

2437
01:49:38.620 --> 01:49:43.330
And like the battle that my dad has been waging

2438
01:49:43.330 --> 01:49:44.560
over the Marshall thing

2439
01:49:44.560 --> 01:49:47.860
is that like Marshall was kind of a racist.

2440
01:49:47.860 --> 01:49:49.439
Because he was a man of that era.

2441
01:49:49.439 --> 01:49:54.439
And so, to what extent do we consider people

2442
01:49:54.580 --> 01:49:56.760
in the context of their time

2443
01:49:56.760 --> 01:50:00.220
and celebrate the great things that they did?

2444
01:50:00.220 --> 01:50:01.900
And when do we need to pay attention

2445
01:50:01.900 --> 01:50:05.390
to things that we have for too long,

2446
01:50:05.390 --> 01:50:07.153
kind of persisted in our blind spots over?

2447
01:50:07.153 --> 01:50:09.670
Well on the Confederate monument front,

2448
01:50:09.670 --> 01:50:11.210
which I thought a lot about,

2449
01:50:11.210 --> 01:50:13.750
and as someone who's fascinated by the Civil War,

2450
01:50:13.750 --> 01:50:16.140
one of the things I've explained

2451
01:50:16.140 --> 01:50:17.930
that like meetings and events and stuff,

2452
01:50:17.930 --> 01:50:22.610
is like you're saying you don't wanna deny

2453
01:50:22.610 --> 01:50:23.870
or forget history.

2454
01:50:23.870 --> 01:50:25.680
The history is important.

2455
01:50:25.680 --> 01:50:28.170
And I go, I agree.

2456
01:50:28.170 --> 01:50:30.760
And this statue is not history.

2457
01:50:30.760 --> 01:50:33.500
This statue is a lie about history, right?

2458
01:50:33.500 --> 01:50:34.960
There's a Confederate monument down the street

2459
01:50:34.960 --> 01:50:36.140
from my bookstore in my office

2460
01:50:36.140 --> 01:50:38.493
that looks like it is coming down.

2461
01:50:39.340 --> 01:50:40.173
But-

2462
01:50:40.173 --> 01:50:41.690
Is that the one you put all this money behind?

2463
01:50:41.690 --> 01:50:42.523
I did, yeah.

2464
01:50:42.523 --> 01:50:44.300
The campaign to get it torn down.

2465
01:50:44.300 --> 01:50:45.800
And it looks like it'll work.

2466
01:50:45.800 --> 01:50:47.890
It looks like it's gonna happen, but you never know.

2467
01:50:47.890 --> 01:50:51.563
But like that statue was put up in 1910.

2468
01:50:53.510 --> 01:50:56.550
Not by grieving widows and orphans of veterans

2469
01:50:56.550 --> 01:50:57.490
of the Civil War.

2470
01:50:57.490 --> 01:50:58.770
By the way, in Bastrop County,

2471
01:50:58.770 --> 01:51:02.740
it was one of the few counties in Texas

2472
01:51:02.740 --> 01:51:04.900
to vote against secession.

2473
01:51:04.900 --> 01:51:07.840
But like that statue was put up

2474
01:51:07.840 --> 01:51:10.620
like two generations after the war

2475
01:51:10.620 --> 01:51:14.780
by people who wanted to deny what the war was about.

2476
01:51:14.780 --> 01:51:17.520
It was a piece of propaganda.

2477
01:51:17.520 --> 01:51:19.000
It was a giant middle finger

2478
01:51:19.000 --> 01:51:21.140
to the federal government, basically.

2479
01:51:21.140 --> 01:51:23.610
It was done over the objections, naturally,

2480
01:51:23.610 --> 01:51:27.980
of the black citizens of the county, of course,

2481
01:51:27.980 --> 01:51:29.563
although it used their money.

2482
01:51:31.050 --> 01:51:35.360
But it was an attempt to tell a false narrative

2483
01:51:35.360 --> 01:51:36.193
about history.

2484
01:51:36.193 --> 01:51:40.910
So, removing it is not erasing history.

2485
01:51:42.230 --> 01:51:45.960
It is allowing the actual history to exist, right?

2486
01:51:45.960 --> 01:51:50.670
Like the lost cause, as we call it,

2487
01:51:50.670 --> 01:51:53.270
is not like a version of history.

2488
01:51:53.270 --> 01:51:55.810
It is propaganda.

2489
01:51:55.810 --> 01:51:56.750
It's a denial.

2490
01:51:56.750 --> 01:52:01.750
It's an attempt to misinform about the worst thing

2491
01:52:01.820 --> 01:52:03.850
that Americans have ever done to each other.

2492
01:52:03.850 --> 01:52:06.510
It's like, hey, remember when we tried

2493
01:52:06.510 --> 01:52:11.290
to destroy the country and 600,000 people died

2494
01:52:12.220 --> 01:52:15.100
because we were not just fighting to defend slavery,

2495
01:52:15.100 --> 01:52:19.090
but we were fighting for our right to expand

2496
01:52:19.090 --> 01:52:24.090
and extend the institution of slavery.

2497
01:52:25.100 --> 01:52:28.720
The statue was put up generations later

2498
01:52:28.720 --> 01:52:29.850
to pretend that wasn't the case.

2499
01:52:29.850 --> 01:52:30.807
Right. I got it.

2500
01:52:30.807 --> 01:52:34.380
And so, as we insist on like not just the removal of them,

2501
01:52:34.380 --> 01:52:38.690
but the putting up of a monument

2502
01:52:38.690 --> 01:52:40.340
to like where lynchings have happened

2503
01:52:40.340 --> 01:52:43.620
or signs about what actually happened,

2504
01:52:43.620 --> 01:52:45.410
we're actually doing the important thing.

2505
01:52:45.410 --> 01:52:46.946
We go to wisdom.

2506
01:52:46.946 --> 01:52:47.779
We're having the courage to face

2507
01:52:47.779 --> 01:52:52.203
the uncomfortable, unpleasant, painful, disturbing,

2508
01:52:54.530 --> 01:52:55.850
ugly truths of history.

2509
01:52:55.850 --> 01:52:57.970
And we have to have the courage to do that.

2510
01:52:57.970 --> 01:52:58.970
I saw a great meme the other day.

2511
01:52:58.970 --> 01:53:01.590
It was like if history,

2512
01:53:01.590 --> 01:53:04.180
like studying history should make you uncomfortable.

2513
01:53:04.180 --> 01:53:05.600
It should make you sad.

2514
01:53:05.600 --> 01:53:07.020
It should make you scared.

2515
01:53:07.020 --> 01:53:08.670
It should make you embarrassed.

2516
01:53:08.670 --> 01:53:10.580
And if the history you're studying

2517
01:53:10.580 --> 01:53:11.970
does not make you feel that,

2518
01:53:11.970 --> 01:53:13.797
you're probably not studying history.

2519
01:53:13.797 --> 01:53:16.650
Right, you're studying some propagandized version

2520
01:53:16.650 --> 01:53:17.483
of history.

2521
01:53:17.483 --> 01:53:19.030
You're being told that you wanna hear.

2522
01:53:19.030 --> 01:53:19.890
Yeah.

2523
01:53:19.890 --> 01:53:22.750
So, you know how Tim Ferriss always asks his guests,

2524
01:53:22.750 --> 01:53:24.180
like if you had a billboard up,

2525
01:53:24.180 --> 01:53:25.870
like what would you say on the billboard?

2526
01:53:25.870 --> 01:53:27.590
Maybe the question for you is,

2527
01:53:27.590 --> 01:53:30.500
if you had the opportunity to erect a monument

2528
01:53:30.500 --> 01:53:33.870
in the nation's capital, like who would that monument be to

2529
01:53:33.870 --> 01:53:36.180
or what would be the saying on it?

2530
01:53:36.180 --> 01:53:37.370
Well, that's my thing.

2531
01:53:37.370 --> 01:53:38.590
I've talked about New Orleans a lot,

2532
01:53:38.590 --> 01:53:39.920
where I lived and I wrote my first book.

2533
01:53:39.920 --> 01:53:42.320
So, they take down this giant statue of Robert E. Lee

2534
01:53:42.320 --> 01:53:43.320
in Lee Circle.

2535
01:53:43.320 --> 01:53:47.910
The big monument in the entrance of the French Quarter.

2536
01:53:47.910 --> 01:53:49.250
Well, that was like three years ago,

2537
01:53:49.250 --> 01:53:50.860
I think four years ago they did this.

2538
01:53:50.860 --> 01:53:54.150
And it's like still stands just like a 90-foot column

2539
01:53:54.150 --> 01:53:54.983
in the air.

2540
01:53:54.983 --> 01:53:55.816
There's nothing there.

2541
01:53:55.816 --> 01:53:58.420
And it's like how many amazing contributions

2542
01:53:58.420 --> 01:54:01.700
to American culture and world culture.

2543
01:54:01.700 --> 01:54:04.510
Yeah, you can't put a statue of Louis Armstrong there

2544
01:54:04.510 --> 01:54:07.670
or like I'd rather see a statue of Lil Wayne there

2545
01:54:07.670 --> 01:54:09.130
than nothing.

2546
01:54:09.130 --> 01:54:11.560
So, like we should be able to decide

2547
01:54:11.560 --> 01:54:13.490
like who we wanna celebrate.

2548
01:54:13.490 --> 01:54:15.100
I think if I had to put something,

2549
01:54:15.100 --> 01:54:17.830
I mean, I like what I was just writing about

2550
01:54:17.830 --> 01:54:21.871
was like we have the Statue of Liberty in the East Coast.

2551
01:54:21.871 --> 01:54:26.020
And I think what the last year has showed us

2552
01:54:26.020 --> 01:54:28.010
is that we don't really have a liberty problem

2553
01:54:28.010 --> 01:54:28.853
in this country.

2554
01:54:29.821 --> 01:54:32.550
If anything, we've got perhaps too much liberty.

2555
01:54:32.550 --> 01:54:33.540
Because people think liberty

2556
01:54:33.540 --> 01:54:36.030
is like now you can own 15 assault rifles

2557
01:54:36.030 --> 01:54:38.360
and not wear a mask or whatever, right?

2558
01:54:38.360 --> 01:54:40.690
Well, I think we have liberty is an issue

2559
01:54:40.690 --> 01:54:43.360
and it's important that we celebrate it

2560
01:54:43.360 --> 01:54:48.360
and that it be monumentized in world's eyes.

2561
01:54:49.550 --> 01:54:50.840
But the idea in this,

2562
01:54:50.840 --> 01:54:52.610
Viktor Frankl actually suggests it

2563
01:54:52.610 --> 01:54:54.480
in "Man's Search for Meaning,"

2564
01:54:54.480 --> 01:54:57.870
that the Statue of Liberty be counterbalanced

2565
01:54:57.870 --> 01:55:02.260
in San Francisco with a statue of responsibility.

2566
01:55:02.260 --> 01:55:04.830
And so, if I could use my powers

2567
01:55:04.830 --> 01:55:08.600
or get people to think about anything, it would be that.

2568
01:55:08.600 --> 01:55:09.433
Yeah.

2569
01:55:09.433 --> 01:55:10.910
And what would that statue look like?

2570
01:55:10.910 --> 01:55:12.010
I don't know.

2571
01:55:12.010 --> 01:55:14.780
Actually, Stephen Covey is supposedly put up

2572
01:55:14.780 --> 01:55:18.307
a good chunk of the money to explore like some designs.

2573
01:55:20.720 --> 01:55:23.370
And it's like a statue of two arms.

2574
01:55:23.370 --> 01:55:26.020
It's like a 90-foot statue of two arms locking like this,

2575
01:55:26.020 --> 01:55:27.010
which I like.

2576
01:55:27.010 --> 01:55:28.160
I like the image of that.

2577
01:55:28.160 --> 01:55:32.100
But I don't know, it strikes me as not iconic

2578
01:55:32.100 --> 01:55:36.290
as the Statue of Liberty.

2579
01:55:36.290 --> 01:55:37.683
Actually, the funny thing is,

2580
01:55:37.683 --> 01:55:39.526
the reason I decided to write the piece

2581
01:55:39.526 --> 01:55:42.850
is that I was reading a book to my kids.

2582
01:55:42.850 --> 01:55:46.570
Dave Eggers wrote a children's book called "Her Right Foot"

2583
01:55:46.570 --> 01:55:48.480
about the Statue of Liberty.

2584
01:55:48.480 --> 01:55:52.920
And did you know that actually like her feet,

2585
01:55:52.920 --> 01:55:55.950
like have you ever looked at the Statue of Liberty's feet?

2586
01:55:55.950 --> 01:55:57.190
Probably, but I can't recall.

2587
01:55:57.190 --> 01:55:58.720
So, one of them, they're raised.

2588
01:55:58.720 --> 01:56:00.680
Like she's walking.

2589
01:56:00.680 --> 01:56:02.180
She's not standing.

2590
01:56:02.180 --> 01:56:03.630
Like when you walk in New York City

2591
01:56:03.630 --> 01:56:06.540
and you see like the characters, they're like,

2592
01:56:06.540 --> 01:56:08.700
that's not what she's doing.

2593
01:56:08.700 --> 01:56:11.890
She's walking out into the harbor.

2594
01:56:11.890 --> 01:56:14.450
You can't see her legs because they're obscured

2595
01:56:14.450 --> 01:56:16.496
by the robes, but she's taking a step forward.

2596
01:56:16.496 --> 01:56:17.650
That kind of changes everything.

2597
01:56:17.650 --> 01:56:21.680
Yes, she's not only is she welcoming people

2598
01:56:21.680 --> 01:56:25.590
into the thing, although the poem comes slightly after

2599
01:56:25.590 --> 01:56:27.870
as actually part of the crowdfunding campaign,

2600
01:56:27.870 --> 01:56:31.280
but like the I think the idea is also that liberty

2601
01:56:31.280 --> 01:56:33.300
is on the move, right?

2602
01:56:33.300 --> 01:56:37.030
Like liberty, it's facing the Atlantic Ocean, right?

2603
01:56:37.030 --> 01:56:39.490
Like out into the world.

2604
01:56:39.490 --> 01:56:44.490
So, I like the idea of I think the best statues

2605
01:56:44.730 --> 01:56:45.960
are of people.

2606
01:56:45.960 --> 01:56:50.370
Although, there's some amazing statues in America,

2607
01:56:50.370 --> 01:56:51.203
monuments that are not people.

2608
01:56:51.203 --> 01:56:55.050
But I would like it to be some sort of embodiment

2609
01:56:55.050 --> 01:56:58.030
of a physical form of a human.

2610
01:56:58.030 --> 01:56:59.080
Well, if you're interested

2611
01:56:59.080 --> 01:57:03.191
in the nation's various monuments

2612
01:57:03.191 --> 01:57:06.190
and you like looking at graves in cemeteries,

2613
01:57:06.190 --> 01:57:07.870
you should follow Ryan on Instagram,

2614
01:57:07.870 --> 01:57:11.330
because it's a never-ending tour of that.

2615
01:57:11.330 --> 01:57:13.260
Every time you go out for a run,

2616
01:57:13.260 --> 01:57:14.840
you never miss a moment to stop,

2617
01:57:14.840 --> 01:57:18.000
take a picture of whatever monument you come across and-

2618
01:57:18.000 --> 01:57:21.581
But that's primarily because my wife hates doing that.

2619
01:57:21.581 --> 01:57:22.521
And so-

2620
01:57:22.521 --> 01:57:25.350
(laughs) I can see how she goes like, "Really?"

2621
01:57:25.350 --> 01:57:28.010
When you're driving cross country, do you have to pull over

2622
01:57:28.010 --> 01:57:29.870
every time you see something like that?

2623
01:57:29.870 --> 01:57:30.703
Yeah.

2624
01:57:30.703 --> 01:57:32.070
So, like if we get into a new city,

2625
01:57:32.070 --> 01:57:33.350
my thing is like I'm gonna go for a run.

2626
01:57:33.350 --> 01:57:36.700
I'm gonna look at all those things, cross them off the list,

2627
01:57:36.700 --> 01:57:37.928
have the little moment with them.

2628
01:57:37.928 --> 01:57:38.761
Do you keep a list?

2629
01:57:38.761 --> 01:57:41.930
No, I just often I'm like, oh, the big thing in this city

2630
01:57:41.930 --> 01:57:43.160
is this thing.

2631
01:57:43.160 --> 01:57:44.440
I wanna see that.

2632
01:57:44.440 --> 01:57:47.356
And then I don't have to drag the whole family to it.

2633
01:57:47.356 --> 01:57:49.360
(laughs) You truly are,

2634
01:57:49.360 --> 01:57:52.580
I've said this before, but you truly are a man out of time.

2635
01:57:52.580 --> 01:57:54.160
I would very much agree with that.

2636
01:57:54.160 --> 01:57:56.850
You really you are a member of the great generation

2637
01:57:56.850 --> 01:58:00.470
at its very like least modern age.

2638
01:58:00.470 --> 01:58:02.762
Perhaps like, you would have been well-suited

2639
01:58:02.762 --> 01:58:06.683
to have been born in 1904 or something.

2640
01:58:07.845 --> 01:58:12.845
Although 1904, then your Spanish flu and both world wars.

2641
01:58:13.370 --> 01:58:14.360
Yeah.

2642
01:58:14.360 --> 01:58:15.240
And the Cold War.

2643
01:58:15.240 --> 01:58:17.640
But in terms of a shared sensibility.

2644
01:58:17.640 --> 01:58:18.670
I do.

2645
01:58:18.670 --> 01:58:21.123
Well, I think what's shared is this,

2646
01:58:22.250 --> 01:58:24.900
I think I actually think those are timeless things,

2647
01:58:24.900 --> 01:58:26.080
and it's part of our problem

2648
01:58:26.080 --> 01:58:29.390
is that we think we're either past certain things

2649
01:58:29.390 --> 01:58:31.030
or like we look back.

2650
01:58:31.030 --> 01:58:33.120
Like I think I say this in the intro of the book,

2651
01:58:33.120 --> 01:58:35.310
is like part of the problem with virtue

2652
01:58:35.310 --> 01:58:38.730
is that we see it as like traditional or old-fashioned.

2653
01:58:38.730 --> 01:58:42.900
And it's like if it's three or 4000 years old,

2654
01:58:42.900 --> 01:58:44.360
it's not old-fashioned.

2655
01:58:44.360 --> 01:58:45.530
It just is.

2656
01:58:45.530 --> 01:58:49.520
Like it's part of who we are.

2657
01:58:49.520 --> 01:58:51.330
It's not like dated.

2658
01:58:51.330 --> 01:58:53.570
It's dateless.

2659
01:58:53.570 --> 01:58:55.810
Right, but as post-modern as we feel

2660
01:58:55.810 --> 01:58:57.130
like we've surpassed it

2661
01:58:57.130 --> 01:59:01.943
or that not only is it old-fashioned, it's anachronistic.

2662
01:59:03.714 --> 01:59:07.700
We're living in a deeper time of enlightenment,

2663
01:59:07.700 --> 01:59:08.810
where we can't be bothered

2664
01:59:08.810 --> 01:59:10.153
with something as archaic as that.

2665
01:59:10.153 --> 01:59:12.420
Well, I think we struggle with

2666
01:59:12.420 --> 01:59:15.690
as we have knocked all those things down,

2667
01:59:15.690 --> 01:59:18.670
we then were like, "Nothing feels meaningful."

2668
01:59:18.670 --> 01:59:21.740
Like I think we are the victims of that.

2669
01:59:21.740 --> 01:59:26.220
We are reaping the consequences

2670
01:59:26.220 --> 01:59:28.720
of like what happens when you tear everything down

2671
01:59:28.720 --> 01:59:31.810
and you replace it with nothing.

2672
01:59:31.810 --> 01:59:36.700
And I think that is one of your copious powers.

2673
01:59:36.700 --> 01:59:39.010
I mean, you're a great writer, you're a prolific writer,

2674
01:59:39.010 --> 01:59:42.510
but to me, you're like a living reminder,

2675
01:59:42.510 --> 01:59:46.310
as a young person, that these things are important.

2676
01:59:46.310 --> 01:59:48.330
And your role or your responsibility

2677
01:59:48.330 --> 01:59:51.410
as kind of this change agent is to remind people

2678
01:59:51.410 --> 01:59:52.773
that these things matter.

2679
01:59:53.740 --> 01:59:54.860
Yeah, I feel like my strength

2680
01:59:54.860 --> 01:59:58.050
is that I can talk about them in a way

2681
01:59:58.050 --> 02:00:00.800
that makes them feel not old-fashioned

2682
02:00:00.800 --> 02:00:03.813
and also makes them feel accessible.

2683
02:00:04.880 --> 02:00:07.130
That's like the trip that I feel like I'm on.

2684
02:00:09.181 --> 02:00:10.830
It's like if you call me a popularizer,

2685
02:00:10.830 --> 02:00:12.000
you're not hurting my feelings.

2686
02:00:12.000 --> 02:00:15.771
That's like you just told me I was successful.

2687
02:00:15.771 --> 02:00:20.730
(laughs) The guy who popularized Stoicism.

2688
02:00:20.730 --> 02:00:23.038
There's worse crimes to be accused of.

2689
02:00:23.038 --> 02:00:24.610
You shared the last time you were here,

2690
02:00:24.610 --> 02:00:27.260
like you were telling Jordan Harbinger about your new book,

2691
02:00:27.260 --> 02:00:28.660
and he was like, oh, is this the one

2692
02:00:28.660 --> 02:00:30.640
where you use examples from history

2693
02:00:30.640 --> 02:00:33.790
and ancient philosophy to explain truths? (laughs)

2694
02:00:33.790 --> 02:00:36.783
Yeah, it's kind of my jam, man. (laughs)

2695
02:00:38.400 --> 02:00:40.620
But it will never go out of style.

2696
02:00:40.620 --> 02:00:42.180
Hopefully not.

2697
02:00:42.180 --> 02:00:43.013
Hopefully not.

2698
02:00:43.013 --> 02:00:44.470
I mean, we'll be in trouble.

2699
02:00:44.470 --> 02:00:49.120
Solzhenitsyn said isn't the first sign of the end,

2700
02:00:49.120 --> 02:00:51.320
a decline in courage?

2701
02:00:51.320 --> 02:00:56.320
And I think decline in virtue is the prolog of the collapse

2702
02:00:59.240 --> 02:01:04.223
of not just empires, but like all movements and moments.

2703
02:01:05.890 --> 02:01:07.800
So, it's heady, it's important.

2704
02:01:07.800 --> 02:01:09.020
Yeah, I mean, the stakes are,

2705
02:01:09.020 --> 02:01:10.420
I think the stakes are high.

2706
02:01:11.530 --> 02:01:13.640
Well, it's always a pleasure to talk to you, my friend.

2707
02:01:13.640 --> 02:01:14.473
It's an honor as always.

2708
02:01:14.473 --> 02:01:17.960
Appreciate your wisdom and your perspective on everything.

2709
02:01:17.960 --> 02:01:19.420
And I'm at your service.

2710
02:01:19.420 --> 02:01:21.130
So, if there's anything I can do to help you,

2711
02:01:21.130 --> 02:01:22.040
please reach out.

2712
02:01:22.040 --> 02:01:24.210
And you're always welcome on this podcast.

2713
02:01:24.210 --> 02:01:25.043
Thank you.

2714
02:01:25.043 --> 02:01:25.876
I appreciate it.

2715
02:01:26.740 --> 02:01:29.580
If you wanna reach out and connect with Ryan,

2716
02:01:29.580 --> 02:01:32.460
you're easy to find, @RyanHoliday everywhere.

2717
02:01:32.460 --> 02:01:36.600
We didn't even talk about your burgeoning YouTube empire

2718
02:01:36.600 --> 02:01:37.670
